      "generated_answer": "**C**\n\nThe standard order in text indexing preprocesses text by first **tokenizing** into words, then **removing stop-words** (common terms like \"the\" or \"and\"), and finally **stemming** the remaining tokens to their root forms. This sequence ensures logical processing: tokens must exist before filtering or normalizing them. Other options reverse or misorder steps, which is inefficient or impossible (e.g., cannot stem or filter without tokens).\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by identifying the standard text preprocessing pipeline for indexing in information retrieval, which processes raw text logically: tokenization first breaks text into individual words or tokens. Next, stop-word elimination removes common, non-informative words (e.g., \"the\", \"is\") from these tokens to reduce noise. Finally, stemming reduces the remaining tokens to their root forms (e.g., \"running\" to \"run\") for normalization.\n\nThis order—Tokenization, Stop-word Elimination, Stemming—matches option C, as confirmed by standard references like \"Introduction to Information Retrieval\" by Manning et al. Options A, B, and D are incorrect because they either precede tokenization (impossible without tokens) or misplace stemming before filtering, which disrupts efficiency. Thus, C represents the most logical and conventional sequence.",
