      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nDynamic indexing with an auxiliary index is ideal for large, frequently changing collections like the internet, as it supports efficient incremental updates without full reindexing. Options A and B suit static or memory-constrained static batches, while C (Map-Reduce) is batch-oriented and less agile for constant changes.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process evaluates the requirements for indexing a large, frequently changing collection like the internet, prioritizing algorithms that support efficient incremental updates.\n\nFirst, each option is assessed: A (Block sort-based) is suited for static batches via sorting large blocks, making it inefficient for frequent changes; B (Single-pass in memory) requires fitting the entire index in memory, which is impractical at internet scale; C (Distributed Map-Reduce) excels in scalability for large data but operates in batches, not handling real-time updates well.\n\nD (Dynamic indexing with auxiliary index) is identified as optimal, as it enables quick additions and modifications through auxiliary structures that merge periodically with the main index, aligning with practices in systems like search engines.\n\nThis selection is confirmed by referencing information retrieval principles, where dynamic approaches address the web's update frequency without full rebuilds.",
