    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_68",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nGiven a 0-indexed string s, permute s to get a new string t such that:\n\nAll consonants remain in their original places. More formally, if there is an index i with 0 <= i < s.length such that s[i] is a consonant, then t[i] = s[i].\nThe vowels must be sorted in the nondecreasing order of their ASCII values. More formally, for pairs of indices i, j with 0 <= i < j < s.length such that s[i] and s[j] are vowels, then t[i] must not have a higher ASCII value than t[j].\n\nReturn the resulting string.\nThe vowels are 'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', and 'u', and they can appear in lowercase or uppercase. Consonants comprise all letters that are not vowels.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: s = \"lEetcOde\"\nOutput: \"lEOtcede\"\nExplanation: 'E', 'O', and 'e' are the vowels in s; 'l', 't', 'c', and 'd' are all consonants. The vowels are sorted according to their ASCII values, and the consonants remain in the same places.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: s = \"lYmpH\"\nOutput: \"lYmpH\"\nExplanation: There are no vowels in s (all characters in s are consonants), so we return \"lYmpH\".\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= s.length <= 10^5\ns consists only of letters of the English alphabet in uppercase and lowercase.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solution(s: str) -> str:\n    vowel_set = set('aeiouAEIOU')\n    vowels = [c for c in s if c in vowel_set]\n    vowels.sort()\n    t = list(s)\n    v_idx = 0\n    for i, c in enumerate(s):\n        if c in vowel_set:\n            t[i] = vowels[v_idx]\n            v_idx += 1\n    return ''.join(t)\n```\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided function solves the problem by first extracting all vowels from the input string `s` into a list, as vowels are the only characters that need rearrangement. A set containing both lowercase and uppercase vowels (`'aeiouAEIOU'`) is used for efficient O(1) lookups to identify vowels.\n\nNext, the list of vowels is sorted using Python's built-in `sort()` method, which orders elements by their ASCII values in non-decreasing order, satisfying the requirement that for any vowel positions i < j, the ASCII value at t[i] is less than or equal to that at t[j].\n\nA list `t` is then created as a mutable copy of `s` to allow in-place modifications. An index `v_idx` tracks the position in the sorted vowels list. The function iterates through each character in `s`: if the character is a vowel (checked via the vowel set), it replaces the corresponding position in `t` with the next vowel from the sorted list (`vowels[v_idx]`), incrementing `v_idx`. Consonants remain unchanged.\n\nFinally, `t` is joined into a string and returned, ensuring consonants stay in their original positions while vowels occupy vowel positions in sorted ASCII order. This approach is efficient with O(n) time for extraction and replacement, and O(k log k) for sorting where k ≤ n is the number of vowels, suitable for n up to 10^5.",
