    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_471",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a array points representing integer coordinates of some points on a 2D plane, where points[i] = [x_i, y_i].\nThe distance between two points is defined as their Manhattan distance.\nReturn the minimum possible value for maximum distance between any two points by removing exactly one point.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: points = [[3,10],[5,15],[10,2],[4,4]]\nOutput: 12\nExplanation:\nThe maximum distance after removing each point is the following:\n\nAfter removing the 0^th point the maximum distance is between points (5, 15) and (10, 2), which is |5 - 10| + |15 - 2| = 18.\nAfter removing the 1^st point the maximum distance is between points (3, 10) and (10, 2), which is |3 - 10| + |10 - 2| = 15.\nAfter removing the 2^nd point the maximum distance is between points (5, 15) and (4, 4), which is |5 - 4| + |15 - 4| = 12.\nAfter removing the 3^rd point the maximum distance is between points (5, 15) and (10, 2), which is |5 - 10| + |15 - 2| = 18.\n\n12 is the minimum possible maximum distance between any two points after removing exactly one point.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: points = [[1,1],[1,1],[1,1]]\nOutput: 0\nExplanation:\nRemoving any of the points results in the maximum distance between any two points of 0.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n3 <= points.length <= 10^5\npoints[i].length == 2\n1 <= points[i][0], points[i][1] <= 10^8",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimum_max_distance(points):\n    n = len(points)\n    s = [p[0] + p[1] for p in points]\n    d_vals = [p[0] - p[1] for p in points]\n    s_sorted = sorted(s)\n    d_sorted = sorted(d_vals)\n    \n    # For s\n    min_s = s_sorted[0]\n    i = 0\n    while i < n and s_sorted[i] == min_s:\n        i += 1\n    num_min_s = i\n    sec_min_s = s_sorted[i] if i < n else min_s\n    \n    max_s = s_sorted[-1]\n    j = n - 1\n    num_max_s = 0\n    while j >= 0 and s_sorted[j] == max_s:\n        num_max_s += 1\n        j -= 1\n    sec_max_s = s_sorted[j] if j >= 0 else max_s\n    \n    # For d_vals\n    min_d = d_sorted[0]\n    i = 0\n    while i < n and d_sorted[i] == min_d:\n        i += 1\n    num_min_d = i\n    sec_min_d = d_sorted[i] if i < n else min_d\n    \n    max_d = d_sorted[-1]\n    j = n - 1\n    num_max_d = 0\n    while j >= 0 and d_sorted[j] == max_d:\n        num_max_d += 1\n        j -= 1\n    sec_max_d = d_sorted[j] if j >= 0 else max_d\n    \n    # Find candidate indices\n    candidates = set()\n    if num_min_s == 1:\n        for k in range(n):\n            if s[k] == min_s:\n                candidates.add(k)\n                break\n    if num_max_s == 1:\n        for k in range(n):\n            if s[k] == max_s:\n                candidates.add(k)\n                break\n    if num_min_d == 1:\n        for k in range(n):\n            if d_vals[k] == min_d:\n                candidates.add(k)\n                break\n    if num_max_d == 1:\n        for k in range(n):\n            if d_vals[k] == max_d:\n                candidates.add(k)\n                break\n    \n    # Full max distance\n    full_rs = max_s - min_s\n    full_rd = max_d - min_d\n    ans = max(full_rs, full_rd)\n    \n    # Check candidates\n    for idx in candidates:\n        # New rs\n        new_min_s = sec_min_s if (s[idx] == min_s and num_min_s == 1) else min_s\n        new_max_s = sec_max_s if (s[idx] == max_s and num_max_s == 1) else max_s\n        new_rs = new_max_s - new_min_s\n        \n        # New rd\n        new_min_d = sec_min_d if (d_vals[idx] == min_d and num_min_d == 1) else min_d\n        new_max_d = sec_max_d if (d_vals[idx] == max_d and num_max_d == 1) else max_d\n        new_rd = new_max_d - new_min_d\n        \n        this_max = max(new_rs, new_rd)\n        if this_max < ans:\n            ans = this_max\n    \n    return ans\n```\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided function solves the problem by leveraging the property that the maximum Manhattan distance in a set of points equals the maximum of the ranges of (x+y) and (x-y) values. To find the minimum possible maximum distance after removing exactly one point, only removals that reduce these ranges need consideration, which occur solely when removing a unique minimum or maximum point in either coordinate system.\n\n1. **Compute transformed coordinates**: Calculate arrays `s` (x+y) and `d_vals` (x-y) for all points.\n\n2. **Sort and analyze extrema**: Sort `s` and `d_vals` to determine `min_s`, `max_s`, `num_min_s`, `num_max_s`, `sec_min_s`, `sec_max_s`, and equivalents for `d_vals`. The second extrema (`sec_*`) are identified from the sorted arrays.\n\n3. **Identify candidate points**: Collect indices of unique extrema (where count == 1) into a set `candidates`. These are the only points whose removal can alter the ranges.\n\n4. **Calculate full maximum distance**: Compute `full_rs = max_s - min_s` and `full_rd = max_d - min_d`, then `full_max = max(full_rs, full_rd)` as the baseline.\n\n5. **Evaluate removals for candidates**: For each candidate index:\n   - Determine new ranges: `new_min_s` updates only if removing the unique `min_s` point, similarly for others.\n   - Compute `new_rs` and `new_rd`, then `this_max = max(new_rs, new_rd)`.\n   - Update answer to the minimum of current answer and `this_max`.\n\n6. **Return minimum**: The result is the smallest maximum distance observed, defaulting to `full_max` if no candidates exist.\n\nThis approach ensures O(n log n) time due to sorting, with additional O(n) for index searches (at most four), efficient for n ≤ 10^5.",
