    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_46",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nThe imbalance number of a 0-indexed integer array arr of length n is defined as the number of indices in sarr = sorted(arr) such that:\n\n0 <= i < n - 1, and\nsarr[i+1] - sarr[i] > 1\n\nHere, sorted(arr) is the function that returns the sorted version of arr.\nGiven a 0-indexed integer array nums, return the sum of imbalance numbers of all its subarrays.\nA subarray is a contiguous non-empty sequence of elements within an array.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [2,3,1,4]\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: There are 3 subarrays with non-zero imbalance numbers:\n- Subarray [3, 1] with an imbalance number of 1.\n- Subarray [3, 1, 4] with an imbalance number of 1.\n- Subarray [1, 4] with an imbalance number of 1.\nThe imbalance number of all other subarrays is 0. Hence, the sum of imbalance numbers of all the subarrays of nums is 3. \n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [1,3,3,3,5]\nOutput: 8\nExplanation: There are 7 subarrays with non-zero imbalance numbers:\n- Subarray [1, 3] with an imbalance number of 1.\n- Subarray [1, 3, 3] with an imbalance number of 1.\n- Subarray [1, 3, 3, 3] with an imbalance number of 1.\n- Subarray [1, 3, 3, 3, 5] with an imbalance number of 2. \n- Subarray [3, 3, 3, 5] with an imbalance number of 1. \n- Subarray [3, 3, 5] with an imbalance number of 1.\n- Subarray [3, 5] with an imbalance number of 1.\nThe imbalance number of all other subarrays is 0. Hence, the sum of imbalance numbers of all the subarrays of nums is 8. \n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 1000\n1 <= nums[i] <= nums.length",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef sumImbalanceNumbers(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    pos = [[] for _ in range(n + 2)]\n    for i, v in enumerate(nums):\n        pos[v].append(i)\n    total = n * (n + 1) // 2\n    \n    def compute_avoid(positions, n):\n        avoid = 0\n        prev = -1\n        for idx in positions:\n            st = prev + 1\n            en = idx - 1\n            if st <= en:\n                lg = en - st + 1\n                avoid += lg * (lg + 1) // 2\n            prev = idx\n        st = prev + 1\n        en = n - 1\n        if st <= en:\n            lg = en - st + 1\n            avoid += lg * (lg + 1) // 2\n        return avoid\n    \n    sum_d = 0\n    for u in range(1, n + 1):\n        positions = pos[u]\n        if positions:\n            avoid = compute_avoid(positions, n)\n            sum_d += total - avoid\n    \n    sum_c = 0\n    for v in range(1, n):\n        A = pos[v]\n        B = pos[v + 1]\n        if not A or not B:\n            continue\n        avoid_a = compute_avoid(A, n)\n        avoid_b = compute_avoid(B, n)\n        # merge A and B\n        merged = []\n        i, j = 0, 0\n        la, lb = len(A), len(B)\n        while i < la and j < lb:\n            if A[i] < B[j]:\n                merged.append(A[i])\n                i += 1\n            else:\n                merged.append(B[j])\n                j += 1\n        merged.extend(A[i:])\n        merged.extend(B[j:])\n        avoid_ab = compute_avoid(merged, n)\n        num_both = total - avoid_a - avoid_b + avoid_ab\n        sum_c += num_both\n    \n    return sum_d - sum_c - total\n```\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided function computes the sum of imbalance numbers across all subarrays by leveraging the relationship: for any subarray, the imbalance equals (number of distinct values D) - (number of consecutive value pairs C, where both v and v+1 are present) - 1. Thus, the total sum is ∑D - ∑C - (total subarrays).\n\n- **Precompute positions**: Create `pos[v]` lists for each value v (1 to n), storing indices where nums[i] == v, enabling O(1) access for later calculations. Total time: O(n).\n\n- **Total subarrays**: Compute `total = n(n+1)/2` in O(1).\n\n- **Compute ∑D**: For each value u, calculate subarrays containing at least one u as `total - subarrays avoiding all pos[u]`. Subarrays avoiding pos[u] are found by summing subarray counts in gaps between sorted positions (before first, between, after last). Time per u: O(|pos[u]|); total: O(n).\n\n- **Compute ∑C**: For each possible consecutive pair (v, v+1) where v=1 to n-1:\n  - If either pos[v] or pos[v+1] is empty, contribute 0.\n  - Otherwise, use inclusion-exclusion: subarrays with ≥1 v and ≥1 (v+1) = `total - avoid(pos[v]) - avoid(pos[v+1]) + avoid(union of pos[v] and pos[v+1])`.\n  - Compute `avoid(S)` for a position list S by summing subarrays in gaps, as before.\n  - Merge sorted pos[v] and pos[v+1] (disjoint, O(|pos[v]| + |pos[v+1]|)) to get union positions.\n  - Time per v: O(|pos[v]| + |pos[v+1]|); total over v: O(n).\n\n- **Final result**: Return `sum_d - sum_c - total`, yielding the required sum in overall O(n) time, efficient for n ≤ 1000.\n\nThis approach avoids enumerating subarrays explicitly, instead counting contributions via position-based avoidance.",
