      "generated_answer": "```python\nclass TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.children = [None] * 26\n        self.min_len = float('inf')\n        self.best_idx = -1\n\n    def update(self, length, idx):\n        if length < self.min_len:\n            self.min_len = length\n            self.best_idx = idx\n        elif length == self.min_len:\n            self.best_idx = min(self.best_idx, idx)\n\ndef longestCommonSuffixIndices(wordsContainer, wordsQuery):\n    root = TrieNode()\n    for i, word in enumerate(wordsContainer):\n        s = word[::-1]\n        L = len(word)\n        node = root\n        node.update(L, i)\n        for char in s:\n            cidx = ord(char) - ord('a')\n            if node.children[cidx] is None:\n                node.children[cidx] = TrieNode()\n            node = node.children[cidx]\n            node.update(L, i)\n    \n    ans = []\n    for q in wordsQuery:\n        q_rev = q[::-1]\n        node = root\n        for char in q_rev:\n            cidx = ord(char) - ord('a')\n            if node.children[cidx] is None:\n                break\n            node = node.children[cidx]\n        ans.append(node.best_idx)\n    return ans\n```\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided Python function solves the problem by constructing a trie from the reversed strings of `wordsContainer` to efficiently identify the longest common suffixes with each reversed query string. Here's the reasoning behind the approach:\n\n1. **Reversing Strings**: Common suffixes in original strings become common prefixes in their reversed versions. Reversing both `wordsContainer` and `wordsQuery` strings transforms the problem into finding the longest common prefix for each reversed query.\n\n2. **Trie Construction**: \n   - Create a `TrieNode` class with 26 children for lowercase letters, `min_len` (minimum length among words passing through the node), and `best_idx` (index of the shortest word with the smallest index among those).\n   - For each word in `wordsContainer` (index `i`, length `L`), insert its reversed version into the trie. Update each node along the insertion path with `L` and `i`, maintaining the best (`min_len`, `best_idx`) pair: update if `L` is smaller, or if equal, take the smaller index.\n\n3. **Query Processing**:\n   - For each `wordsQuery[j]`, reverse it to `q_rev`.\n   - Traverse the trie from the root following characters in `q_rev` until no matching child exists. The stopping node represents the deepest common prefix (longest common suffix in original strings).\n   - All words passing through this node share exactly this maximum common prefix length. The precomputed `best_idx` at this node is the required index (shortest length, then earliest index).\n\n4. **Efficiency**:\n   - Trie building: O(sum of lengths of `wordsContainer`) due to single traversal per character.\n   - Query processing: O(sum of lengths of `wordsQuery`) for traversals.\n   - Space: O(sum of lengths of `wordsContainer`) for trie nodes.\n   - This meets constraints with total time O(10^6) operations.\n\nThis ensures correct selection per the tie-breaking rules: longest suffix first, then shortest length, then smallest index.",
