    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_127",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nGiven three integers a, b, and n, return the maximum value of (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) where 0 <= x < 2^n.\nSince the answer may be too large, return it modulo 10^9 + 7.\nNote that XOR is the bitwise XOR operation.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: a = 12, b = 5, n = 4\nOutput: 98\nExplanation: For x = 2, (a XOR x) = 14 and (b XOR x) = 7. Hence, (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) = 98. \nIt can be shown that 98 is the maximum value of (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) for all 0 <= x < 2^n.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: a = 6, b = 7 , n = 5\nOutput: 930\nExplanation: For x = 25, (a XOR x) = 31 and (b XOR x) = 30. Hence, (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) = 930.\nIt can be shown that 930 is the maximum value of (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) for all 0 <= x < 2^n.\nExample 3:\n\nInput: a = 1, b = 6, n = 3\nOutput: 12\nExplanation: For x = 5, (a XOR x) = 4 and (b XOR x) = 3. Hence, (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) = 12.\nIt can be shown that 12 is the maximum value of (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) for all 0 <= x < 2^n.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n0 <= a, b < 2^50\n0 <= n <= 50",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nMOD = 10**9 + 7\n\ndef maximum_product(a: int, b: int, n: int) -> int:\n    low_mask = (1 << n) - 1\n    A = (a >> n) << n\n    B = (b >> n) << n\n    c = (a ^ b) & low_mask\n    P = A\n    Q = B\n    for k in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        ck = (c >> k) & 1\n        bit = 1 << k\n        if ck == 0:\n            P += bit\n            Q += bit\n        else:\n            if Q >= P:\n                P += bit\n            else:\n                Q += bit\n    return (P * Q) % MOD\n```\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided function computes the maximum value of (a XOR x) * (b XOR x) for 0 ≤ x < 2^n by constructing optimal values for p = a XOR x and q = b XOR x through a greedy bit-by-bit approach on the lowest n bits.\n\n- **Precompute fixed components**: Extract the higher bits (≥ n) of a and b as A = a & ~(2^n - 1) and B = b & ~(2^n - 1), using bit shifts for precision. Compute c = (a XOR b) & (2^n - 1), which fixes the XOR relationship in the lowest n bits: q_low = p_low XOR c.\n\n- **Initialize**: Set P = A and Q = B, representing the values before deciding the lowest n bits.\n\n- **Greedy bit decisions from highest to lowest bit (k = n-1 to 0)**:\n  - Extract the k-th bit of c (ck).\n  - If ck == 0, both p and q can have the k-th bit set to 1 (by setting y_k = 1 where y = p_low), maximizing both: add 2^k to P and Q.\n  - If ck == 1, exactly one of p or q gets the k-th bit as 1:\n    - Adding 2^k to P yields an increment of 2^k * Q.\n    - Adding 2^k to Q yields an increment of 2^k * P.\n    - Choose the option with the larger increment: add to P if Q ≥ P (prioritizing addition to the smaller or equal value to leverage the larger multiplier), otherwise add to Q.\n\n- **Result**: After processing all bits, P and Q are the optimal values achieving the maximum product. Compute (P * Q) % (10^9 + 7).\n\nThis greedy strategy is optimal because decisions prioritize the immediate largest product increase at each bit, and lower bits contribute less than 2^k total, preserving the choice's benefit. Verification with examples confirms correctness.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 420,
        "output_tokens": 597,
        "total_tokens": 7615
      },
      "provider": "azure",
