      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maxStrongPairXor(nums):\n    max_xor = 0\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        for j in range(len(nums)):\n            x = nums[i]\n            y = nums[j]\n            if abs(x - y) <= min(x, y):\n                max_xor = max(max_xor, x ^ y)\n    return max_xor\n```\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided function solves the problem by exhaustively checking all possible pairs in the array, including pairs of the same element, to identify strong pairs and compute their bitwise XOR values.\n\n- A strong pair requires |x - y| ≤ min(x, y), which ensures the larger number is at most twice the smaller one.\n- Given the constraints (n ≤ 50 and nums[i] ≤ 100), a brute-force O(1 * n²) approach is efficient, as it performs at most 2500 operations.\n- The function initializes `max_xor` to 0, covering the base case of self-pairs (x ^ x = 0).\n- It iterates over all indices i and j, computes x = nums[i] and y = nums[j], and checks the strong pair condition.\n- If the condition holds, it updates `max_xor` with the maximum of its current value and (x ^ y).\n- This guarantees finding the global maximum XOR among all valid strong pairs, as verified by the examples:\n  - For [1,2,3,4,5], pairs like (3,4) yield 3 ^ 4 = 7, the maximum.\n  - For [10,100], only self-pairs are valid, yielding 0.\n  - For [5,6,25,30], (25,30) yields 25 ^ 30 = 7, exceeding other pairs like (5,6) = 3.",
