      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maximumSumOfSquares(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    cnt = [0] * 32\n    for num in nums:\n        for b in range(32):\n            if (num & (1 << b)) != 0:\n                cnt[b] += 1\n    ans = 0\n    for r in range(1, k + 1):\n        x = 0\n        for b in range(32):\n            if cnt[b] >= r:\n                x += (1 << b)\n        ans = (ans + (x * x % MOD)) % MOD\n    return ans\n```\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning behind the provided function stems from analyzing the problem's operations and constraints. The key insight is that for each bit position b (0 to 31), the number of array elements with that bit set, denoted cnt[b], remains invariant under the allowed operations. This is because operations either preserve or relocate bit instances without changing their total count.\n\nTo maximize the sum of squares of the k largest elements, assign bits to k hypothetical \"top\" elements greedily: process bits from highest to lowest and allocate each bit's instances to the current largest elements. This greedy strategy simplifies to an exact formula: the r-th largest element (1-based index r from 1 to k) receives all bits b where cnt[b] >= r. This ensures the highest-value bits are concentrated in the fewest elements possible, respecting the multiplicity constraints and maximizing variance for sum of squares.\n\nThe function implements this as follows:\n- Compute cnt[b] by iterating over all elements and bits.\n- For each rank r (1 to k), calculate the value x_r as the sum of (1 << b) for all b where cnt[b] >= r.\n- Compute the sum of (x_r)^2 modulo 10^9 + 7.\n\nThis assignment is achievable through operations, as it corresponds to nested bit sets (S_1 ⊇ S_2 ⊇ ... ⊇ S_k), which can be constructed by selectively merging bits into target elements. The time complexity is O(n * 32 + k * 32), efficient for the constraints.",
