      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom typing import List\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef maximumElementSum(nums: List[int]) -> int:\n    def get_kernel(x: int) -> int:\n        kernel = 1\n        # handle 2\n        cnt = 0\n        while x % 2 == 0:\n            cnt += 1\n            x //= 2\n        if cnt % 2 == 1:\n            kernel *= 2\n        # odd primes\n        p = 3\n        while p * p <= x:\n            cnt = 0\n            while x % p == 0:\n                cnt += 1\n                x //= p\n            if cnt % 2 == 1:\n                kernel *= p\n            p += 2\n        if x > 1:\n            kernel *= x\n        return kernel\n    \n    n = len(nums)\n    group_sums = defaultdict(int)\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        k = get_kernel(i)\n        group_sums[k] += nums[i - 1]\n    return max(group_sums.values()) if group_sums else 0\n```\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning behind the provided function is based on the observation that a subset of indices forms a complete set if the product of every pair of indices in the subset is a perfect square. This occurs when all indices in the subset share the same parity vector of prime exponents, represented by their square-free kernel.\n\nTo solve this:\n1. Compute the square-free kernel for each index i from 1 to n. The kernel is obtained by multiplying the primes that have odd exponents in i's prime factorization.\n2. Group the nums values by these kernels using a dictionary, summing the values within each group.\n3. The maximum sum among these groups represents the largest possible element-sum of a complete subset, as indices with the same kernel satisfy the pairwise product condition, and mixing kernels violates it.\n\nThe `get_kernel` helper function efficiently computes the kernel by trial division, handling the prime 2 separately and then odd primes up to sqrt(x). This approach is efficient given n ≤ 10^4.\n\nThe function processes the array in O(n * sqrt(n)) time, which is acceptable for the constraints.",
