    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string word and an integer k.\nA substring s of word is complete if:\n\nEach character in s occurs exactly k times.\nThe difference between two adjacent characters is at most 2. That is, for any two adjacent characters c1 and c2 in s, the absolute difference in their positions in the alphabet is at most 2.\n\nReturn the number of complete substrings of word.\nA substring is a non-empty contiguous sequence of characters in a string.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: word = \"igigee\", k = 2\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: The complete substrings where each character appears exactly twice and the difference between adjacent characters is at most 2 are: igigee, igigee, igigee.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: word = \"aaabbbccc\", k = 3\nOutput: 6\nExplanation: The complete substrings where each character appears exactly three times and the difference between adjacent characters is at most 2 are: aaabbbccc, aaabbbccc, aaabbbccc, aaabbbccc, aaabbbccc, aaabbbccc.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= word.length <= 10^5\nword consists only of lowercase English letters.\n1 <= k <= word.length",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef count_complete_substrings(word: str, k: int) -> int:\n    n = len(word)\n    ans = 0\n    i = 0\n    while i < n:\n        start = i\n        while i < n - 1 and abs(ord(word[i]) - ord(word[i + 1])) <= 2:\n            i += 1\n        # segment from start to i inclusive\n        seg = word[start:i + 1]\n        nseg = len(seg)\n        if nseg >= k:\n            a_ord = ord('a')\n            for m in range(1, 27):\n                l = m * k\n                if l > nseg:\n                    break\n                freq = [0] * 26\n                for p in range(l):\n                    cc = ord(seg[p]) - a_ord\n                    freq[cc] += 1\n                # check first window\n                is_good = True\n                for f in freq:\n                    if f != 0 and f != k:\n                        is_good = False\n                        break\n                if is_good:\n                    ans += 1\n                # slide the rest\n                for st in range(1, nseg - l + 1):\n                    out_cc = ord(seg[st - 1]) - a_ord\n                    freq[out_cc] -= 1\n                    in_cc = ord(seg[st + l - 1]) - a_ord\n                    freq[in_cc] += 1\n                    is_good = True\n                    for f in freq:\n                        if f != 0 and f != k:\n                            is_good = False\n                            break\n                    if is_good:\n                        ans += 1\n        i += 1\n    return ans\n```\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided Python function solves the problem by first identifying maximal \"good segments\" where all adjacent characters satisfy the alphabet difference condition (|ord(c1) - ord(c2)| ≤ 2). Substrings crossing these breaks cannot be complete, so valid substrings lie entirely within these segments.\n\nFor each good segment of length `nseg`:\n- Only substrings of length `l = m * k` (for `m = 1` to `26`, since at most 26 distinct characters) are candidates, as the length must equal the number of distinct characters times `k`.\n- For each `l`, a sliding window of fixed length `l` is used to check all possible substrings:\n  - Initialize frequency array for the first window `[0, l-1]`.\n  - Verify if every frequency is exactly `0` or `k` by iterating over the 26 possible characters.\n  - Slide the window by removing the outgoing character and adding the incoming one, then recheck the condition.\n- This ensures all frequency constraints are met, and the adjacent condition is guaranteed within the segment.\n\nTime complexity: O(26 * n) per segment due to 26 possible `m` values, each with O(n) windows and O(26) checks per window, plus O(∑ l) ≤ O(26 * n) for initializations, totaling O(n) overall since segments partition the string. This is efficient for n ≤ 10^5.",
