      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimumOperations(num: str) -> int:\n    n = len(num)\n    s = num\n    targets = {\"00\", \"25\", \"50\", \"75\"}\n    max_len = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            pair = s[i] + s[j]\n            if pair in targets:\n                max_len = max(max_len, i + 2)\n    has_zero = '0' in s\n    if has_zero:\n        max_len = max(max_len, 1)\n    return n - max_len\n```\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided function solves the problem by maximizing the length of a subsequence that forms a number divisible by 25, thereby minimizing deletions. A number is divisible by 25 if it is 0 (empty string or single \"0\") or ends with \"00\", \"25\", \"50\", or \"75\".\n\n- Define the target endings as a set: {\"00\", \"25\", \"50\", \"75\"}.\n- Initialize `max_len` to 0 (for the empty subsequence, which is 0).\n- Iterate over all pairs of indices `i < j`:\n  - Form the pair `s[i] + s[j]`.\n  - If it matches a target, the longest subsequence ending with this pair includes all digits from index 0 to `i-1` (length `i`), plus `s[i]` and `s[j]`, for a total length of `i + 2`.\n  - Update `max_len` with the maximum such length.\n- Check if '0' exists in `s`; if so, update `max_len` to at least 1 (for the single \"0\" subsequence).\n- The minimum operations is `n - max_len`, as it equals the digits not included in the longest valid subsequence.\n\nThis approach ensures all cases are covered, including short strings and edge cases like no valid pairs (falling back to 0 or 1). The O(1 + n^2) time complexity is efficient given n ≤ 100.",
