      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef countInterestingSubarrays(nums: List[int], modulo: int, k: int) -> int:\n    freq = {0: 1}\n    current = 0\n    ans = 0\n    for num in nums:\n        is_good = 1 if (num % modulo == k) else 0\n        current = (current + is_good) % modulo\n        target = (current - k + modulo) % modulo\n        if target in freq:\n            ans += freq[target]\n        freq[current] = freq.get(current, 0) + 1\n    return ans\n```\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided function solves the problem by transforming it into a subarray sum problem modulo `modulo`. \n\n- Define an array where each element `a[i]` is 1 if `nums[i] % modulo == k` (a \"good\" element), otherwise 0. This tracks the count of good elements in subarrays.\n- Compute prefix sums modulo `modulo`, where `prefix[i+1]` is the sum of the first `i+1` elements of `a` modulo `modulo`.\n- A subarray from index `L` to `R` has sum `prefix[R+1] - prefix[L]`. It is interesting if this sum ≡ `k` (mod `modulo`), or equivalently, `prefix[L] ≡ prefix[R+1] - k` (mod `modulo`).\n- Use a hash map `freq` to store the frequency of each prefix sum modulo `modulo` encountered so far. Initialize `freq[0] = 1` for the empty prefix.\n- Iterate through the array:\n  1. For each index `i` (corresponding to right endpoint `R = i`), update `current` to `prefix[i+1]`.\n  2. Compute the target residue: `target = (current - k + modulo) % modulo`.\n  3. Add `freq[target]` to the answer, as it counts valid left endpoints `L ≤ i`.\n  4. Increment `freq[current]` to include this prefix for future subarrays.\n- This ensures O(1) queries per iteration, achieving O(n) time overall, suitable for `n ≤ 10^5`.\n- The modulo operations handle the circular arithmetic correctly, and the hash map manages up to `n+1` distinct residues efficiently.",
