    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimumOperations(s1: str, s2: str, s3: str) -> int:\n    min_len = min(len(s1), len(s2), len(s3))\n    lcp_len = 0\n    for i in range(min_len):\n        if s1[i] == s2[i] == s3[i]:\n            lcp_len = i + 1\n        else:\n            break\n    if lcp_len == 0:\n        return -1\n    return (len(s1) - lcp_len) + (len(s2) - lcp_len) + (len(s3) - lcp_len)\n```\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided function solves the problem by identifying the longest common prefix (LCP) of the three strings, as this determines the longest possible equal string achievable through rightmost deletions. The minimum operations are then calculated by summing the deletions needed to trim each string to this LCP length.\n\n- Compute `min_len` as the minimum length among s1, s2, and s3, since the LCP cannot exceed this.\n- Iterate through indices from 0 to `min_len - 1`, checking if characters at position `i` match across all three strings (`s1[i] == s2[i] == s3[i]`). Update `lcp_len` to `i + 1` if they match; break otherwise.\n- After the loop, `lcp_len` holds the length of the LCP.\n- If `lcp_len == 0`, no common prefix of length at least 1 exists, so return -1, as strings cannot be made equal (operations cannot reduce strings below length 1).\n- Otherwise, compute total operations as `(len(s1) - lcp_len) + (len(s2) - lcp_len) + (len(s3) - lcp_len)`, which minimizes deletions by maximizing the common prefix length.\n\nThis approach works because deletions only remove suffixes, so equal strings must be common prefixes. Using the longest such prefix minimizes total operations, given the constraints. Time complexity is O(min(len(s1), len(s2), len(s3))), efficient for lengths up to 100.",
