      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef lengthOfLongestSubsequence(nums: List[int], target: int) -> int:\n    dp = [-1] * (target + 1)\n    dp[0] = 0\n    for num in nums:\n        for s in range(target, num - 1, -1):\n            if dp[s - num] >= 0:\n                dp[s] = max(dp[s], dp[s - num] + 1)\n    return dp[target] if dp[target] >= 0 else -1\n```\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided function solves the problem using dynamic programming to find the longest subsequence summing to the target. The array `dp` is defined such that `dp[s]` represents the length of the longest subsequence summing to `s`. Initialize `dp[0] = 0` (empty subsequence sums to 0) and all other `dp[s] = -1` (indicating impossible).\n\nFor each `num` in `nums`, iterate over sums `s` from `target` down to `num` to avoid using the same element multiple times:\n- If `dp[s - num] >= 0`, update `dp[s] = max(dp[s], dp[s - num] + 1)` to consider adding `num` to a valid subsequence summing to `s - num`.\n\nThis backward iteration ensures each element is considered at most once and in original order, maintaining the subsequence property.\n\nAfter processing all elements, `dp[target]` holds the longest length if `>= 0`; otherwise, return `-1`.\n\nThe time complexity is O(n * target) and space complexity is O(target), both efficient given constraints.",
