      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport bisect\n\nclass SegmentTree:\n    def __init__(self, size):\n        self.n = size\n        self.tree = [-1] * (4 * (size + 1))\n\n    def update(self, idx, val):\n        self._update(1, 1, self.n, idx, val)\n\n    def _update(self, node, start, end, idx, val):\n        if start == end:\n            self.tree[node] = max(self.tree[node], val)\n            return\n        mid = (start + end) // 2\n        if idx <= mid:\n            self._update(2 * node, start, mid, idx, val)\n        else:\n            self._update(2 * node + 1, mid + 1, end, idx, val)\n        self.tree[node] = max(self.tree[2 * node], self.tree[2 * node + 1])\n\n    def query(self, left, right):\n        if left > right:\n            return -1\n        return self._query(1, 1, self.n, left, right)\n\n    def _query(self, node, start, end, left, right):\n        if right < start or end < left:\n            return -1\n        if left <= start and end <= right:\n            return self.tree[node]\n        mid = (start + end) // 2\n        lmx = self._query(2 * node, start, mid, left, right)\n        rmx = self._query(2 * node + 1, mid + 1, end, left, right)\n        return max(lmx, rmx)\n\ndef maximumSumQueries(nums1, nums2, queries):\n    n = len(nums1)\n    points = [(nums1[i], nums2[i]) for i in range(n)]\n    points.sort(key=lambda p: -p[0])\n    \n    all_b = sorted(set(nums2))\n    m = len(all_b)\n    if m == 0:\n        return [-1] * len(queries)\n    \n    qlist = [(queries[i][0], queries[i][1], i) for i in range(len(queries))]\n    qlist.sort(key=lambda t: -t[0])\n    \n    answer = [-1] * len(queries)\n    ptr = 0\n    seg = SegmentTree(m)\n    \n    for qx, qy, qidx in qlist:\n        while ptr < n and points[ptr][0] >= qx:\n            a, b = points[ptr]\n            val = a + b\n            r = bisect.bisect_left(all_b, b) + 1\n            seg.update(r, val)\n            ptr += 1\n        \n        pos = bisect.bisect_left(all_b, qy)\n        if pos < m:\n            L = pos + 1\n            mx = seg.query(L, m)\n            if mx != -1:\n                answer[qidx] = mx\n    \n    return answer\n```\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided solution solves the problem efficiently in O((n + q) log n) time by processing points and queries offline after sorting.\n\n1. **Sort points and queries**: Points (pairs from nums1 and nums2) are sorted by nums1 values in descending order. Queries are sorted by x_i in descending order to process from largest x to smallest, ensuring points are added progressively as their nums1 meets the threshold.\n\n2. **Coordinate compression for nums2**: Collect unique values from nums2, sort them ascendingly into `all_b`. This allows indexing nums2 values (b_j) into ranks (1 to m) for the segment tree, where m ≤ n.\n\n3. **Segment tree for maximum sum queries**: The segment tree maintains the maximum sum (nums1[j] + nums2[j]) at each rank corresponding to nums2[j]. It supports:\n   - Updates: When adding a point, compute its rank via binary search on `all_b` and update the tree with its sum.\n   - Range maximum queries: For a query y_i, find the lowest rank L where all_b[L-1] ≥ y_i using bisect_left, then query max sum in [L, m].\n\n4. **Processing loop**:\n   - Traverse sorted queries.\n   - For each query with x_i, add all points where nums1[j] ≥ x_i (using a pointer on sorted points) to the segment tree.\n   - Query the segment tree for max sum where nums2[j] ≥ y_i. If no valid range or max is -1 (unupdated), result is -1.\n\n5. **Correctness**:\n   - All added points satisfy nums1[j] ≥ x_i.\n   - The segment tree ensures the queried range covers only nums2[j] ≥ y_i among added points.\n   - Initializing to -1 handles cases with no valid j.\n\nThis approach avoids brute force by leveraging sorting and a dynamic data structure for efficient range maximum queries.",
