    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9280",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Can a stream cipher have perfect secrecy?\n\nOptions: \nA. Yes, if the PRG is really “secure”\nB. No, because perfect secrecy is not possible in any cipher\nC. Yes, if the stream cipher is implemented correctly\nD. No, since the key is shorter than the message\nE. Yes, every cipher has perfect secrecy\nF. Yes, if the key is longer than the message\nG. No, only block ciphers can have perfect secrecy\nH. Yes, if the key is used only once\nI. No, since the key is reused for the stream cipher\nJ. No, there are no ciphers with perfect secrecy\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nStream ciphers use a short fixed-length key to generate a long keystream via a PRG, making the key shorter than the message and preventing perfect secrecy (which requires key length ≥ message length per Shannon). Secure PRGs provide computational security, not information-theoretic perfect secrecy. The one-time pad achieves perfect secrecy but is not a typical stream cipher.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by recalling that perfect secrecy requires the key length to be at least as long as the message, as per Shannon's theorem, ensuring the ciphertext reveals no information about the plaintext. Stream ciphers generate a long keystream from a short, fixed-length key (e.g., 128-256 bits) using a pseudorandom generator (PRG), which is shorter than typical message lengths, violating this requirement. Thus, option D (\"No, since the key is shorter than the message\") is correct, while others fail: A, C, E, F, and H assume security or conditions not inherent to stream ciphers; B, G, I, and J misstate facts about ciphers or perfect secrecy achievability (e.g., one-time pad).",
      "success": true,
