    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9091",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Maximize the entropy $H(X)$ of a non-negative integer-valued random variable $X$, taking values from 0 to infinity, subject to the constraint $E(X)=1$. Use base 2 logarithm to evaluate $H(X)$.\n\nOptions: \nA. 4.0\nB. 2.0\nC. 3.0\nD. 1.0\nE. 4.5\nF. 2.5\nG. 3.5\nH. 0.5\nI. 5.0\nJ. 1.5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nThe maximum entropy distribution for a non-negative integer-valued random variable \\(X\\) with \\(E(X)=1\\) is the geometric distribution \\(P(X=k)=(1/2)^{k+1}\\) for \\(k=0,1,2,\\dots\\).  \nIts entropy is \\(H(X)=\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty P(X=k) (k+1) = \\sum_{m=1}^\\infty m (1/2)^m = 2\\) bits.  \nThis achieves the maximum by the maximum entropy principle for the given constraints.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning identifies the geometric distribution as the maximum entropy distribution for a non-negative integer-valued random variable with fixed mean \\(E(X)=1\\). For this distribution, solve \\( \\mu = (1-p)/p = 1 \\), yielding \\( p = 1/2 \\), so \\( P(X=k) = (1/2)^{k+1} \\) for \\( k = 0, 1, 2, \\dots \\).  \n\nEntropy is computed as \\( H(X) = -\\sum P(X=k) \\log_2 P(X=k) \\). Substituting \\( P(X=k) = 2^{-(k+1)} \\) gives \\( \\log_2 P(X=k) = -(k+1) \\), so \\( H(X) = \\sum (1/2)^{k+1} (k+1) \\).  \n\nReindexing with \\( m = k+1 \\) yields \\( H(X) = \\sum_{m=1}^\\infty m (1/2)^m \\), and using the formula \\( \\sum m r^m = r / (1-r)^2 \\) for \\( |r| < 1 \\) and \\( r = 1/2 \\), results in \\( (1/2) / (1/2)^2 = 2 \\) bits. This confirms the value matches option B and is maximal per the maximum entropy principle.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
