    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**J**\n\nIn a complete K-ary tree of depth N, the total number of nodes is \\((K^{N+1} - 1)/(K-1)\\), and nonterminal (internal) nodes number \\((K^N - 1)/(K-1)\\). The ratio of nonterminal to total nodes is \\((K^N - 1)/(K^{N+1} - 1)\\), which approximates \\(1/K\\) for large N. This matches option J best among the choices.\n\n\\boxed{J}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by recalling the structure of a complete K-ary tree of depth N, with levels from 0 to N. The total number of nodes is the sum of a geometric series: \\(\\sum_{i=0}^{N} K^i = \\frac{K^{N+1} - 1}{K-1}\\). Nonterminal nodes consist of all nodes except the leaves at level N, so their count is \\(\\sum_{i=0}^{N-1} K^i = \\frac{K^N - 1}{K-1}\\). The ratio of nonterminal nodes to total nodes is then \\(\\frac{(K^N - 1)/(K-1)}{(K^{N+1} - 1)/(K-1)} = \\frac{K^N - 1}{K^{N+1} - 1}\\). For large N, the dominant terms yield \\(\\frac{K^N}{K^{N+1}} = \\frac{1}{K}\\), making option J the best approximation, as other options either depend on N or do not match this asymptotic behavior.",
