      "generated_answer": "**H**\n\nThe fundamental periods of the components are \\(T_1 = 2/3\\) and \\(T_2 = 2/5\\). The fundamental period \\(T\\) of the sum is the least common multiple, which is 2 (since \\(T = 3 \\times 2/3 = 5 \\times 2/5 = 2\\)). No smaller positive value among the options works for both.\n\n\\boxed{H}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by identifying the fundamental periods of the individual cosine components. For \\(\\cos(3\\pi t)\\), the angular frequency is \\(3\\pi\\), so the period is \\(T_1 = 2\\pi / 3\\pi = 2/3\\). For \\(\\cos(5\\pi t - 0.5\\pi)\\), the angular frequency is \\(5\\pi\\) (phase shift does not affect the period), so \\(T_2 = 2\\pi / 5\\pi = 2/5\\).\n\nThe period of the sum is the least common multiple (LCM) of \\(T_1\\) and \\(T_2\\), found by solving for the smallest \\(T\\) such that \\(T = m \\cdot (2/3) = n \\cdot (2/5)\\) for positive integers \\(m\\) and \\(n\\). This yields \\(5m = 3n\\); the smallest solution is \\(m=3\\), \\(n=5\\), giving \\(T = 3 \\cdot (2/3) = 2\\) or \\(T = 5 \\cdot (2/5) = 2\\).\n\nTo confirm \\(T=2\\) is fundamental, check smaller candidates like 1: \\(1 / (2/3) = 1.5\\) (not an integer) and \\(1 / (2/5) = 2.5\\) (not an integer), so no smaller common multiple exists among the options. Alternatively, the frequencies are 1.5 Hz and 2.5 Hz, with greatest common divisor 0.5 Hz, confirming period \\(1 / 0.5 = 2\\). This matches option H.",
