      "generated_answer": "**F**\n\nTo adapt EM for MAP estimates, modify only the M-step to maximize the expected complete log-likelihood plus the log-prior on the parameters, while the E-step remains unchanged as it computes expectations under the posterior over latents.  \nNo other steps require changes.\n\n\\boxed{F}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by recalling that the standard EM algorithm maximizes the expected complete log-likelihood in the E-step and M-step to find maximum likelihood estimates (MLE). For MAP estimates, the objective shifts to maximizing the log-posterior, which equals the log-likelihood plus the log-prior on the parameters. Thus, the E-step, which computes the expectation of the complete log-likelihood under the posterior over latent variables given current parameters, remains unchanged, as it does not involve the prior on parameters. The M-step must be modified to maximize the expected complete log-likelihood plus the log-prior term. Among the options, this corresponds to \"F. Maximization,\" as no other steps like initialization, convergence criteria, or the E-step alone require alteration.",
