      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nThis encryption scheme is the standard PRF-based randomized counter-like encryption, which is vulnerable to a CPA distinguishing attack with advantage roughly q/|R| (where q is the number of prior encryptions of a known message like 0): if the challenge r repeats a prior r, the adversary computes c* ⊕ c_j to recover mb ⊕ 0 and distinguish m0=0 from m1=some fixed delta. This attack is information-theoretic and succeeds whenever repeats occur. Thus, IND-CPA (semantic security under CPA) holds only if |R| is large enough that repeats are negligible w.h.p. for any poly-time adversary.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided answer, B, is correct because the encryption scheme E(k, m) = (r ← R, F(k, r) ⊕ m) faces a structural vulnerability under CPA when r repeats. In the IND-CPA game, an adversary first obtains q encryptions of a known message m_j = 0, yielding ciphertexts (r_j, c_j = F(k, r_j)). For the challenge encryption of m_b (where m0 = 0 and m1 = δ ≠ 0), if the challenge r* equals some prior r_j (with probability ≈ q / |R|), the adversary computes d = c* ⊕ c_j; d = 0 if b=0, and d = δ if b=1, allowing perfect distinction conditioned on collision. This yields overall distinguishing advantage Θ(q / |R|), which is negligible only if |R| is sufficiently large (e.g., superpolynomial in the security parameter) to ensure r never repeats with overwhelming probability across polynomially many queries. Thus, E is semantically secure under CPA precisely under the condition in option B, independent of F's PRF properties.",
