    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "TLS protects against server impersonation via server certificate verification during the handshake. TLS also protects against replay of earlier client-sent bytes (TLS records) due to per-connection sequence numbers and MAC integrity checks, which reject duplicates or out-of-order records. Thus, it protects against both attacks.\n\n\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning evaluates whether TLS, under the given conditions, protects against each specified attack.\n\nFirst, for attack 2 (attacker impersonating the server): TLS requires the client to verify the server's certificate during the handshake, which was successfully completed. This authentication ensures the server's identity, preventing impersonation.\n\nNext, for attack 1 (attacker replaying bytes that a client sent earlier): The TLS record protocol uses an implicit sequence number that increments per record and is included in MAC computations. Receivers reject records with duplicate or out-of-order sequence numbers, blocking replays within the connection; across connections, new keys from the Diffie-Hellman exchange further invalidate old records.\n\nThus, TLS protects against both attacks, corresponding to option A (True, True).",
