      "generated_answer": "I. Correct: Larger pages reduce the number of pages, thus shrinking page table size.  \nII. Correct: Smaller pages minimize internal fragmentation waste (average ~half page).  \nIII. Correct: Disk seek/rotation dominate (page-independent), so larger pages efficiently transfer more data per access.  \nAll apply, so G.\n\n\\boxed{G}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning evaluates each statement against standard paging system principles. Statement I holds true because larger page sizes result in fewer pages for the same address space, thereby reducing the overall size of the page table. Statement II is accurate as smaller pages limit internal fragmentation, where unused space within a page averages about half a page, making waste proportional to page size. Statement III is valid since disk access time is primarily driven by seek time and rotational latency—factors independent of page length—allowing larger pages to transfer more data per access without significantly increasing total time. Thus, all three statements apply, corresponding to option G.",
