    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nHash tables excel at exact-match operations (insert, delete, search, frequency counts, etc.) with O(1) average-case time, supporting options A, B, C, E, F, G, and even H by enabling fast lookups instead of linear scans. However, they do not support efficient range searches (option D), as keys are unordered, requiring O(n) time to scan all entries in the worst/average case. Balanced trees (e.g., BSTs) are better suited for ranges.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process evaluates each option to identify where hash tables fail to provide an efficient average-case solution, defined as O(1) or O(n) time for the problem size.\n\nHash tables support constant-time average-case operations for exact-match tasks: A (key comparison via direct hashing), B (distinct count using a hash set), C (dynamic dictionary operations), E (symbol table lookups), F (frequency counts with a hash map), G (uniqueness via existence or count checks), and H (replacing sequential search with O(1) lookups).\n\nFor D (range search), hash tables store keys in unordered buckets, necessitating a full scan of all elements (O(n) average case), which is inefficient compared to ordered structures like binary search trees.\n\nThus, D is the exception, as hash tables cannot contribute an efficient average-case solution for range queries.",
