    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**I**\n\nKruskal's or Prim's algorithm solves minimum spanning tree (I) using a standard greedy approach by selecting the smallest eligible edges without cycles. Maximum clique (II) is NP-hard and lacks an optimal standard greedy solution. Maximum flow (III) requires augmenting path methods like Ford-Fulkerson, which involve residual graphs and are not purely greedy.\n\n\\boxed{I}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning evaluates each problem against standard greedy algorithms, which make locally optimal choices without backtracking.\n\nFor I, minimum spanning tree in an undirected graph with positive-integer edge weights can be solved by Kruskal's algorithm (sorting edges by weight and adding the smallest without cycles) or Prim's algorithm (growing a tree from a starting vertex by adding the minimum-weight edge), confirming it as solvable by a standard greedy approach.\n\nFor II, finding a maximum clique in an undirected graph is NP-hard, with no standard greedy algorithm guaranteeing an optimal solution, as greedy heuristics may produce suboptimal results.\n\nFor III, maximum flow in a directed graph with positive-integer edge capacities requires algorithms like Ford-Fulkerson or Edmonds-Karp, which iteratively find augmenting paths in the residual graph and involve revisiting capacities, making it not solvable by a standard greedy method.\n\nThus, only I qualifies, corresponding to option I.",
