    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9163",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let G = (V, E) be a connected, undirected graph, and let a and b be two distinct vertices in V. Let P_1 be the problem of finding a shortest simple path between a and b, and let P_2 be the problem of finding a longest simple path between a and b. Which of the following statements about P_1 and P_2 is true?\n\nOptions: \nA. Both P_1 and P_2 are known to not be solvable in polynomial time.\nB. Both P_1 and P_2 can be solved in polynomial time.\nC. P_1 is not known to be solvable in polynomial time but P_2 can be solved in polynomial time.\nD. P_2 can be solved in polynomial time and P_1 is known to not be solvable in polynomial time.\nE. P_2 can be solved in polynomial time but P_1 is not known to be solvable in polynomial time.\nF. It is known that either P_1 or P_2 can be solved in polynomial time.\nG. P_1 can be solved in polynomial time but P_2 is not known to be solvable in polynomial time.\nH. It is not known whether either P_1 or P_2 can be solved in polynomial time.\nI. P_1 is known to not be solvable in polynomial time but P_2 can be solved in polynomial time.\nJ. P_1 can be solved in polynomial time and P_2 is known to not be solvable in polynomial time.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**G**\n\nP1 (shortest simple path) can be solved in polynomial time using BFS, which runs in O(|V| + |E|) time and naturally produces simple paths.  \nP2 (longest simple path) is NP-hard, so it is not known to be solvable in polynomial time (as this would imply P = NP).  \nNo other option matches this distinction.\n\n\\boxed{G}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by analyzing the problems: P1 involves finding the shortest simple path in an unweighted, undirected, connected graph, while P2 involves finding the longest simple path between the same vertices.  \n\nBFS solves P1 in O(|V| + |E|) time, as it explores the graph level by level and guarantees a simple shortest path, confirming P1 is solvable in polynomial time.  \n\nP2 is NP-hard, as the decision version (existence of a simple path of length at least k) is NP-complete via reduction from Hamiltonian path; thus, P2 is not known to be solvable in polynomial time, since resolving this would require settling P = NP.  \n\nAmong the options, only G accurately states that P1 can be solved in polynomial time while P2 is not known to be solvable in polynomial time, eliminating alternatives like H (which claims neither is known) or J (which incorrectly asserts P2 is known not solvable).",
