      "generated_answer": "**A**\n\nFor the fully interconnected network, the number of links is \\( \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\) and the worst-case path length is 1, so the figure of merit is \\( \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\).  \nFor the ring network, the number of links is \\( n \\) and the worst-case path length is \\( \\frac{n}{2} \\) (even \\( n > 2 \\)), so the figure of merit is \\( \\frac{n^2}{2} \\).  \nThe ratio (fully / ring) is \\( \\frac{n(n-1)/2}{n^2/2} = \\frac{n-1}{n} \\).\n\n\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by calculating the figure of merit for each network. For the fully interconnected network, the number of bidirectional links is \\( \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\) (the number of edges in a complete graph), and the worst-case path length is 1, yielding a figure of merit of \\( \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\). For the ring network, the number of links is \\( n \\) (one cycle edge per processor), and the worst-case path length is \\( \\frac{n}{2} \\) for even \\( n > 2 \\), resulting in a figure of merit of \\( n \\times \\frac{n}{2} = \\frac{n^2}{2} \\). The ratio of the fully interconnected figure of merit to the ring figure of merit is then \\( \\frac{\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}}{\\frac{n^2}{2}} = \\frac{n(n-1)}{n^2} = \\frac{n-1}{n} \\), which matches option A.",
