      "generated_answer": "**C**\n\nWith only 3 bits (allowing 0-7 unsigned), 5 (101) + 3 (011) = 8 (1000), which overflows and wraps to 0 (000). This is a classic integer overflow error.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by noting that 3 bits can represent unsigned integers from 0 to 7. Adding 5 (binary 101) and 3 (binary 011) yields 8 (binary 1000), which exceeds the 3-bit capacity. In modular arithmetic for 3 bits (modulo 8), 8 wraps around to 0 (binary 000), indicating an overflow error, which directly matches option C as the best explanation. Other options, such as H (result not negative) or F (binary calculation is standard), do not fit as precisely.",
