      "generated_answer": "I and III are decidable: I by simulating \\(n\\) steps; III by simulating up to a finite bound of configurations within at most \\(n-1\\) contiguous cells (possible positions \\(-(n-2)\\) to \\(n-2\\)) to detect expansion or cycling. II is undecidable, as checking if a 1 is ever printed after \\(n\\) steps reduces to an undecidable reachability problem on unbounded tape.\n\n\\boxed{J}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning evaluates the decidability of each statement by considering simulation strategies for Turing machine M on a blank tape.\n\nFor I, the computation lasts at least n steps if it does not halt within n-1 steps. Simulate M for exactly n steps: if it halts earlier, output no; otherwise, output yes. This always terminates, making I decidable.\n\nFor II, first simulate n steps to check if it lasts at least n steps (as in I). If so, determine from the configuration after n steps whether a 1 is ever written later. This requires checking reachability in an unbounded configuration space, which is undecidable (e.g., reducible to the halting problem via modifications that write 1 only on halting).\n\nFor III, note that visited cells form a contiguous interval [L, R] starting from 0, with size R - L + 1 non-decreasing. If the size never reaches n, it stays ≤ m = n-1 forever, confining activity to positions -(m-1) to m-1 (2m-1 cells). Compute S = |Q| × (2m-1) × 3^(2m-1) configurations. Simulate up to S+1 steps: if size ≥ n or halts, decide accordingly; if not, a configuration repeat implies cycling within bounds, so output no. This terminates, making III decidable.\n\nThus, only I and III are decidable, corresponding to option J.",
