      "generated_answer": "**A. False**\n\nSurprising someone who has gone no-contact (even \"sorta\") with a large, uninvited family group of 13+ people heavily disregards their boundaries and could make them feel ambushed or unsafe. While intentions are good and there's no bad blood, no-contact signals a need for space, and a surprise visit risks overwhelming him on his birthday rather than celebrating it. Respecting his wishes means asking first or sending a gift/card instead.\n\n\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided AI-generated answer, \\boxed{A}, concludes that the action is not morally acceptable (False), based on AITA-style judgment where YTA aligns with boundary violations. The reasoning evaluates the brother's no-contact status as a clear signal for space, despite occasional texts and shared love, making an unannounced visit with 13+ family members disrespectful and potentially harmful. This prioritizes consent and emotional boundaries over good intentions, as overwhelming a low-contact relationship could cause distress rather than joy.",
