    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**G**\n\nThe Babylonian captivity (or Exile) began in 586 BCE with the destruction of the First Temple in Jerusalem by Nebuchadnezzar II and the major deportation of Judeans to Babylon. While earlier deportations occurred (e.g., 597 BCE), 586 BCE marks the primary start. Other options refer to unrelated events like the Assyrian conquest (722 BCE) or Roman destruction (70 CE).\n\n\\boxed{G}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe thought process begins by identifying the Babylonian captivity as the period when Babylon conquered Judah and exiled Jews under Nebuchadnezzar II, with the key event being the destruction of the First Temple in Jerusalem. This aligns with the standard historical date of 586 BCE, corresponding to option G. Options are then evaluated: A (333 BCE) relates to Alexander the Great; B (1066 CE) to the Battle of Hastings; C (535 BCE) to the exile's approximate end; D (515 BCE) to the Second Temple's completion; E (1914 CE) and F (1492 CE) to modern events; H (722 BCE) to the Assyrian conquest of the Northern Kingdom; I (70 CE) and J (70 BCE) to Roman-era events. Although earlier deportations occurred in 597 BCE, 586 BCE is the traditional start for the main exile phase, confirming G as the most accurate choice among the options.",
