    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The first Jaina temples appeared during the Mauryan period, around the 3rd century BCE, aligning with early monumental architecture in India and the conversion of rulers like Chandragupta Maurya to Jainism. Earlier dates like the 5th century BCE predate significant surviving temple structures. Later options correspond to more developed Jain architecture.\n\n\\boxed{H}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by recognizing that Jainism originated in the 6th-5th century BCE with Mahavira, but temple construction developed later amid early Indian monumental architecture. The Mauryan period (322-185 BCE), particularly the 3rd century BCE, marks the emergence of the first Jaina temples, as evidenced by historical associations with rulers like Chandragupta Maurya, who converted to Jainism, and the context of early rock-cut and structural developments. Among the options, H (Third century BCE) is the most plausible, as earlier choices like D (Fifth century BCE) precede reliable archaeological evidence for temples, while later ones (e.g., C, E) align with subsequent expansions rather than initial appearances.",
