    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**E**\n\nAccording to Parfit in *Reasons and Persons*, both Taurek and Lewis reject summing pains across different people, assuming a \"sum of pain\" requires all pains to be felt by a single person (the Separate Persons Objection).  \nOther options like temporal or causal unity (e.g., I, F, H) are not the focus of Parfit's critique.  \nThis aligns with Parfit's aggregation arguments against such views.\n\n\\boxed{E}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by identifying the question's focus: Parfit's critique in *Reasons and Persons*, where he addresses the Separate Persons Objection raised by Taurek and Lewis regarding the aggregation of pains.  \n\nIt then recalls that both philosophers deny summing pains across individuals, assuming pains can only form a \"sum\" if experienced by a single person, as separate persons' sufferings are not aggregable.  \n\nOptions are evaluated sequentially: A–D, F–I fail to match this critique (e.g., A involves avoidability, I temporal unity), while E directly aligns; J is dismissed as overly broad.",
