    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "I. blue\n\nAfterimages follow the opponent-process theory of color vision, where prolonged staring at one color fatigues its opponent channel, producing the complementary color. Yellow's opponent is blue, so the afterimage on white paper appears blue.  \n\\boxed{I}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided answer derives from the opponent-process theory of color vision, which explains afterimages as the result of fatiguing specific color opponent channels. Staring at yellow fatigues the blue-yellow opponent channel on the yellow side, leading to an afterimage dominated by blue when viewing a neutral white background. This aligns with standard complementary color pairs, where yellow's complement is blue, as confirmed by color theory and common visual experiments.",
      "success": true,
