    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2528",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: When the corpus callosum is cut in a right-handed patient, often the patient\n\nOptions: \nA. Cannot name objects placed in the right hand if the patient can see them\nB. cannot recognize objects placed in the right hand\nC. cannot name objects placed in the left hand if the patient cannot see them\nD. cannot carry out well-learned motor tasks with the left hand\nE. shows a decline in verbal intelligence\nF. loses the ability to speak\nG. Cannot name objects placed in the right hand if the patient cannot see them\nH. cannot carry out well-learned motor tasks with the right hand\nI. shows a decline in spatial intelligence\nJ. can no longer write with their right hand\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In split-brain patients with a severed corpus callosum, tactile information from the left hand is processed in the non-verbal right hemisphere, preventing naming of unseen objects placed there. The right hand sends information to the verbal left hemisphere, allowing naming. Thus, option C is correct.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by noting that severing the corpus callosum prevents communication between the brain's hemispheres, and in right-handed individuals, language functions reside primarily in the left hemisphere. Tactile sensations from the left hand are processed exclusively in the right hemisphere, which lacks language capabilities, so patients cannot verbally name objects placed in the left hand when vision is blocked. This directly matches option C, while other options are ruled out: for instance, right-hand sensations reach the left hemisphere (eliminating A, B, G, H, and J), motor tasks remain intact (eliminating D and H), and no general cognitive declines occur (eliminating E, F, and I).",
