      "generated_answer": "**J**\n\nNarcotics (opioids) are primarily used to control moderate to severe pain. Common treatments for their abuse include medication-assisted therapy (e.g., methadone or buprenorphine) to manage physiological dependence, alongside behavioral therapies and often residential rehabilitation programs. Other options misstate either the medical use or treatment approaches.\n\n\\boxed{J}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by identifying the primary medical use of narcotics, which are opioids primarily employed for controlling moderate to severe pain, directly matching option J. It then evaluates treatments for abuse, noting that standard protocols involve medication-assisted therapies like methadone to address physiological addiction, combined with social interventions such as live-in residential facilities, as specified in J. Other options are eliminated because they inaccurately describe narcotics' uses (e.g., anxiety in A, sleep in B, weight loss in C) or treatments (e.g., meditation in A, counseling alone in G), confirming J as the most accurate choice based on pharmacological and clinical standards.",
