    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Dan read a list of 30 vocabulary words only once. If he is typical and shows the serial position effect, we would expect that the words he remembers two days later are\n\nOptions: \nA. at the end of the list\nB. words that were repeated in the list\nC. the longest words in the list\nD. words related to a specific topic\nE. the last ten words he read\nF. the shortest words in the list\nG. at the beginning of the list\nH. in the middle of the list\nI. distributed throughout the list\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The serial position effect shows better recall for items at the beginning (primacy effect) and end (recency effect) of a list, but after a long delay like two days, the recency effect fades as short-term memory decays, leaving primarily the beginning items remembered due to transfer to long-term memory. Thus, for a typical person, the words best remembered would be at the beginning of the list.  \n\n\\boxed{G}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
