    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2428",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If you rub your eyes long and hard enough, you would eventuallysee some type of visual pattern. Explain this phenomenon.\n\nOptions: \nA. The rubbing action generates light inside the eye\nB. Pressure on the eye temporarily alters the shape of the cornea, leading to distorted visual signals.\nC. The mechanical stimulation from rubbing triggers a reflex that causes the pupils to dilate and create patterned visuals.\nD. Friction from rubbing generates heat, which causes the illusion of light patterns.\nE. It happens due to the production of a natural hallucinogen when pressure is applied\nF. The pressure causes the release of stored light energy in the eye\nG. The pressure exerted on the eye stimulates the receptor nerve cells at the back of the retina, which normally respond to light, and their response to this pressure, when communicated to the visual area of the brain, produce the experience of light.\nH. Rubbing the eyes causes a temporary change in blood flow to the visual cortex, resulting in visual phenomena.\nI. The rubbing motion activates latent photoreceptor cells which produce the visual patterns.\nJ. The optic nerve is temporarily disoriented by the rubbing, resulting in random visual patterns.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**G**\n\nThe phenomenon is known as phosphenes, where mechanical pressure directly stimulates retinal photoreceptor cells (normally activated by light), firing signals to the visual cortex that the brain interprets as patterns or light. Option G accurately describes this process. Other options involve incorrect mechanisms like light generation, heat, or blood flow changes.\n\n\\boxed{G}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by recognizing the phenomenon as phosphenes, which occur due to mechanical stimulation of the retina's photoreceptor cells. These cells, typically responsive to light, fire action potentials when pressure is applied, sending signals to the visual cortex that the brain perceives as visual patterns. Among the options, G precisely matches this mechanism by stating that pressure stimulates retinal receptor nerve cells, leading to the experience of light, while alternatives like A, B, D, and others describe inaccurate processes such as internal light generation or corneal distortion.",
