    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2544",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Although a man watched in horror as his wife and children were killed by a speeding truck as they crossed the street, he has no memory of the event and gets upset when people tell him he must remember. The man is most likely suffering from\n\nOptions: \nA. major depressive disorder\nB. antisocial personality disorder\nC. bipolar disorder\nD. dissociative amnesia\nE. conversion disorder\nF. obsessive-compulsive disorder\nG. schizophrenia\nH. generalized anxiety disorder\nI. post-traumatic stress disorder\nJ. panic disorder\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D. dissociative amnesia**\n\nDissociative amnesia involves the sudden inability to recall important autobiographical information, typically traumatic, which matches the man's lack of memory for witnessing the deaths. Other options like PTSD (I) involve intrusive memories rather than total amnesia, while depression (A) or anxiety disorders (H, J) do not feature selective memory loss. Conversion disorder (E) manifests as physical symptoms, not cognitive ones like forgetting an event.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by identifying the key symptoms: the man's inability to recall a traumatic event despite witnessing it, combined with distress when reminded, which aligns directly with dissociative amnesia's core feature of selective memory loss for personal, traumatic information. Each option is then systematically evaluated: major depressive disorder (A) involves persistent sadness without amnesia; antisocial personality disorder (B) features disregard for others; bipolar disorder (C) entails mood swings; conversion disorder (E) produces unexplained physical symptoms; obsessive-compulsive disorder (F) includes obsessions and compulsions; schizophrenia (G) involves psychosis; generalized anxiety disorder (H) centers on excessive worry; panic disorder (J) on sudden attacks; and post-traumatic stress disorder (I), while trauma-related, typically includes intrusive memories rather than complete amnesia. This elimination confirms dissociative amnesia (D) as the most precise match, as it specifically addresses trauma-induced memory gaps without overlapping symptoms from other disorders.",
