    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2126",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Dr. Ramchandran found that his patients who brushed their teeth after lunch had 1/20 the number of cavities in their teeth as those who didn't. After interviewing the dentist, a local newswriter reports that brushing teeth after lunch prevents cavities. Based on the dentist's research, which of the following statements is true?\n\nOptions: \nA. The writer's statement would be justified if at least half of the patients brushed their teeth after lunch.\nB. If a minimum of 200 patients were studied, the writer's statement is justified.\nC. No matter how many participants, the writer's statement is not justified.\nD. The writer's statement would only be justified if no patients brushed their teeth after lunch.\nE. The writer's statement could only be justified if the study was conducted over a prolonged period of time.\nF. At least 100 of the patients needed to have brushed their teeth after lunch for the writer's statement to be justified.\nG. If all the patients were studied, the writer's statement is justified.\nH. If at least 100 patients were studied, the writer's statement is justified.\nI. If only the patients who brushed their teeth after lunch were studied, the writer's statement is justified.\nJ. If a minimum of 500 patients were studied, the writer's statement is justified.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "C. No matter how many participants, the writer's statement is not justified.\n\nThe study shows only correlation between brushing after lunch and fewer cavities, not causation, as no experimental controls or randomization are mentioned. Sample size or subgroup details do not address this fundamental flaw. Thus, the causal claim is unjustified regardless of study parameters.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided AI-generated answer identifies option C as correct because the dentist's study demonstrates correlation between brushing teeth after lunch and reduced cavities, not causation. Observational studies like this cannot establish that brushing prevents cavities, as confounding variables (e.g., overall oral hygiene habits) may explain the difference. Regardless of sample size, participant numbers, or study subgroups—as outlined in options A, B, F, G, H, I, and J—the leap to causation remains invalid, making C the only accurate statement.",
