    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by recalling that the human eye has two types of photoreceptors: cones, which handle color vision and require brighter light, and rods, which enable vision in dim light but only in shades of black, white, and gray. Option B accurately describes this distinction, stating that cones need more light for activation while rods function in low light without color perception. Other options are incorrect: A is too vague, C misrepresents retinal function, D focuses on adaptation without addressing photoreceptors, E and J reverse the roles of rods and cones, F inaccurately claims both require high light, G oversimplifies cone behavior, H is overly general, and I wrongly attributes color detection to rods. Thus, B provides the most precise explanation matching established visual physiology.",
