    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A stroke that affects the posterior region of the corpus callosum, thus disconnecting the visual centers of the brain from the language centers of the brain, may result in this syndrome:\n\nOptions: \nA. Aphasia\nB. Broca's aphasia\nC. Hemianopsia\nD. Alexia without agraphia\nE. Apraxia\nF. Agraphia without alexia\nG. Dysarthria\nH. Prosopagnosia\nI. Wernicke's aphasia\nJ. Agnosia\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nA stroke in the posterior corpus callosum (splenium) disconnects the right occipital visual cortex from left-hemisphere language areas, causing alexia (inability to read) while sparing writing ability. This classic disconnection syndrome is known as alexia without agraphia. Other options involve different lesions or symptoms, such as general aphasia or visual field defects.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided answer identifies option D, alexia without agraphia, based on the stroke's location in the posterior corpus callosum, which disconnects visual centers (occipital lobes) from language centers (left hemisphere). This lesion prevents visual input from the right visual cortex from crossing to left language areas via the splenium, resulting in impaired reading while preserving writing, as motor and language production pathways remain intact. Other options are ruled out because they involve different brain regions or symptoms: for example, aphasias (A, B, I) stem from perisylvian lesions, hemianopsia (C) from optic pathway damage, and agraphia without alexia (F) from opposite hemispheric disruptions.",
