      "generated_answer": "**G**\n\nWork sample tests (G) have the strongest job-relatedness via content validity, making them most defensible under Uniform Guidelines even with adverse impact. Cognitive ability tests (I) and others like integrity (F) often face greater scrutiny due to higher disparate impact risks. Polygraphs (J) are largely banned for pre-employment use.\n\n\\boxed{G}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning starts by identifying the context of employment testing under laws like Title VII and Uniform Guidelines, where adverse impact requires proof of job-relatedness and business necessity. Among options, work sample tests (G) are prioritized due to their direct simulation of job tasks, providing strong content validity that courts readily accept as defensible. Other tests, such as cognitive ability (I) with frequent disparate impact, integrity (F) with validity concerns, or polygraph (J) which is often prohibited, face higher legal risks, confirming G as the optimal choice.",
