    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2430",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of doing field researchand laboratory research with human subjects.\n\nOptions: \nA. Laboratory research results are less valid than field research because they are always artificial.\nB. Laboratory research is always preferable over field research\nC. Field research is always better than laboratory research\nD. Field research is less ethical than laboratory research because it involves real-world scenarios.\nE. Field research yields quicker results than laboratory research.\nF. Field research focuses on real people in real-world situations offering more impact but less control and laboratory research provides controlled situations with less impact but more control. There is a basic tradeoff between impact and control in these two methods.\nG. Field research provides more control than laboratory research\nH. Laboratory research can be conducted in any environment, while field research is limited to specific settings.\nI. Field research typically requires more resources than laboratory research.\nJ. Laboratory research allows for studying larger groups than field research does.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**F**\n\nField research offers high ecological validity and real-world impact but sacrifices control over variables, while laboratory research provides precise control for causal inference at the cost of artificiality and lower generalizability. This captures the core tradeoff without overstating one method's superiority. Other options are inaccurate or overly absolute.\n\n\\boxed{F}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided answer, F, is selected because the question requires discussing advantages and disadvantages of field and laboratory research with human subjects, and F accurately outlines the key tradeoff: field research provides high real-world impact and ecological validity but lower control, whereas laboratory research offers greater control for isolating variables but reduced external validity. This balanced comparison aligns with standard research methodology principles, avoiding absolutes or inaccuracies in other options. For instance, A incorrectly deems lab results always less valid, B and C favor one method universally, and G reverses the control dynamic, making F the most comprehensive and correct choice.",
