    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A. Statistical nonsignificance under low power\n\nThis outcome is least informative because low power increases the risk of a Type II error, making it unclear whether the null hypothesis is true or the study simply lacked sensitivity to detect an effect. In contrast, the other options provide clearer evidence either for or against the null hypothesis.  \n\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning evaluates each option's informativeness based on statistical significance and power. Statistical significance indicates rejection of the null hypothesis, while power reflects the ability to detect a true effect. Option A (nonsignificance under low power) is least informative due to high risk of Type II error, yielding inconclusive results. Option B (significance under low power) suggests a potentially strong effect despite limitations. Option C (nonsignificance under high power) provides strong evidence for the null. Option D (significance under high power) offers reliable evidence against the null. Thus, A represents the classic inconclusive scenario.",
