    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_108",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nH ≡ (I · ~J)\nK ≡ ~H\n~(K ⊃ J) / I ≡ J\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when I, H, and K are true and J is false\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when I and H are true and J and K are false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when K is true and I, H, and J are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning uses an indirect truth table to test validity by assuming all premises are true and the conclusion false, checking for contradiction. The premises are: (1) H ≡ (I ∧ ¬J), (2) K ≡ ¬H, and (3) ¬(K ⊃ J), which simplifies to K ∧ ¬J, so K is true and J is false. The conclusion I ≡ J is false only if I and J differ; with J false from premise 3, I must be true for falsity.\n\nAssuming premises true and conclusion false yields: J false, K true (premise 3), so H false (from premise 2: K true ≡ ¬H). Then premise 1 requires H ≡ (I true ∧ ¬J true), or H ≡ true, but H is false, creating a contradiction. Thus, no assignment satisfies premises true and conclusion false, proving validity.\n\nOptions B, C, and D fail as counterexamples: B falsifies premise 2, C falsifies premise 3, and D makes the conclusion true.",
      "success": true,
