      "generated_answer": "**C**\n\n\"Neither math nor science\" means Dylan likes neither (not math and not science), which is equivalent to ~(M ∨ S) by De Morgan's law. Option C directly captures this. Options B and D mean \"not both,\" allowing liking one subject.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe sentence \"Dylan likes neither math nor science\" translates to not liking math and not liking science, symbolized as ~M ∧ ~S in propositional logic, where M means \"likes math\" and S means \"likes science.\" By De Morgan's law, ~M ∧ ~S is equivalent to ~(M ∨ S), matching option C. Options A (M ∨ S) means likes at least one; B (~(M • S)) and D (~M ∨ ~S) both mean not both (equivalent to ~(M ∧ S)), allowing liking one subject, which does not fit. Thus, C provides the precise symbolization.",
