    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_148",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In the event of bankruptcy, what is the order of payment of bonds, common stock, and preferred stock?\n\nOptions: \nA. Preferred stockholders, common stockholders, bondholders\nB. Bondholders, common stockholders, preferred stockholders\nC. Preferred stockholders, bondholders, common stockholders\nD. Common stockholders, bondholders, preferred stockholders\nE. Bondholders and common stockholders equally, then preferred stockholders\nF. Preferred stockholders, then bondholders and common stockholders equally\nG. Bondholders equally with preferred stockholders, then common stockholders\nH. Common stockholders, preferred stockholders, bondholders\nI. Bondholders, preferred stockholders, common stockholders\nJ. Common stockholders and preferred stockholders equally, then bondholders\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**I**\n\nIn bankruptcy liquidation, bondholders (creditors) have the highest priority and are paid first, followed by preferred stockholders, and then common stockholders last. This reflects the standard hierarchy where debt ranks above equity. Option I matches this order precisely.\n\n\\boxed{I}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by identifying the standard priority in bankruptcy liquidation: bondholders, as creditors, receive payment first due to their debt obligations. Next, it recognizes that among equity holders, preferred stockholders have priority over common stockholders in asset distribution. The options are then systematically evaluated, confirming that only option I—Bondholders, preferred stockholders, common stockholders—aligns with this established hierarchy, while others incorrectly reorder or equalize priorities.",
