  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_530",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: You have just redesigned your kitchen. The new floor dimensions are 7'6\" × 11'8\". Now you want to cover the floor with linoleum, which sells for $1.79 per running foot in 6' width, what is the best way to cover the floor? How much will it cost?\n\nOptions: \nA. $48.60\nB. $26.85\nC. $30.99\nD. $42.56\nE. $29.99\nF. $38.40\nG. $31.50\nH. $23.94\nI. $20.12\nJ. $35.70\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The floor measures 7.5 ft by 11⅔ ft (11.67 ft). The best way to cover it is to lay two 6-ft-wide strips along the 7.5-ft dimension (total linear footage: 2 × 7.5 ft = 15 ft), as this covers the 11.67-ft width (12 ft total > 11.67 ft) at lower cost than the alternative orientation. Cost: 15 ft × $1.79/ft = $26.85.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by converting dimensions: 7'6\" to 7.5 feet and 11'8\" to 11⅔ feet (approximately 11.67 feet). To minimize cost with 6-foot-wide linoleum priced at $1.79 per running foot, evaluate orientations: laying strips along the 7.5-foot dimension requires two 6-foot-wide strips, each 7.5 feet long, for a total of 15 linear feet, as 12 feet of width exceeds 11.67 feet. This yields a cost of 15 × $1.79 = $26.85, matching option B and being less than the alternative (two strips along 11.67 feet, totaling about 23.33 linear feet at higher cost).",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 193,
        "output_tokens": 272,
        "total_tokens": 3253
      },
      "provider": "azure",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.00017460000000000002,
    "accuracy": 1.0
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_227",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Mrs. Bracken is a widow who maintains a home for herself, her dependent father, and her two children. (a) What filing status should she claim? (b) How many exemptions is she allowed?\n\nOptions: \nA. Single, four exemptions\nB. Qualifying widow(er), three exemptions\nC. Qualifying widow(er), six exemptions\nD. Married filing jointly, three exemptions\nE. Qualifying widow(er), five exemptions\nF. Married filing jointly, four exemptions\nG. Married filing separately, two exemptions\nH. Single, three exemptions\nI. Head of household, three exemptions\nJ. Head of household, four exemptions\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**J. Head of household, four exemptions**\n\nMrs. Bracken qualifies for Head of Household status as an unmarried widow maintaining a home for herself and qualifying dependents (two children and father). She claims one personal exemption for herself plus three dependency exemptions (father and two children), totaling four. Qualifying widow(er) is not indicated without a specified timeframe since her spouse's death.\n\n\\boxed{J}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process evaluates Mrs. Bracken's situation against tax filing status rules and exemption allowances. First, her status as a widow who is unmarried and maintains a home for herself, a dependent father, and two children aligns with Head of Household requirements, as it involves paying more than half the cost of keeping up a home for qualifying persons (qualifying children and a dependent parent). Qualifying Widow(er) is ruled out due to the lack of specified timing (spouse's death within two prior years). For exemptions, she receives one personal exemption for herself and one each for her three dependents (two children and father), totaling four, which matches option J. This combination best fits the provided details among the options.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 186,
        "output_tokens": 223,
        "total_tokens": 1410
      },
      "provider": "azure",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.0001487,
    "accuracy": 1.0
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_640",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose Q is a measure of output, K is a measure of capital stock in use, and L is a measure of the size of the employed labor force. Q =AK^\\betaL^\\gamma is a much used form of production function. When \\beta + \\gamma = 1, it is known as a Cobb-Douglas production function. This function has many convenient properties: Marginal products of both labor and capital are positive if \\beta and \\gamma are. If \\beta + \\gamma = 1, the function has constant returns to scale. That is, if both inputs are increased in like proportion, output will increase in that same proportion. A doubling of both inputs, for example, will result in doubled output. Discuss how the concept of linear regression might be applied to estimate \\beta and \\gamma .\n\nOptions: \nA. Polynomial regression\nB. Linear progression\nC. Quantile regression\nD. Ridge regression\nE. Logistic regression\nF. Exponential regression\nG. Lasso regression\nH. Multiple regression\nI. Single regression\nJ. Non-linear regression\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**H**\n\nTo estimate β and γ in the Cobb-Douglas production function Q = A K^β L^γ, take natural logs to get ln(Q) = ln(A) + β ln(K) + γ ln(L), which is linear in the logged variables. This setup allows the use of multiple linear regression, with ln(Q) as the dependent variable and ln(K), ln(L) as independent variables. Other options like polynomial, logistic, or regularization methods do not fit this linearized form.\n\n\\boxed{H}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by recognizing that the Cobb-Douglas production function Q = A K^β L^γ is nonlinear, but taking the natural logarithm transforms it into ln(Q) = ln(A) + β ln(K) + γ ln(L), yielding a linear equation. This equation features ln(Q) as the dependent variable and ln(K) and ln(L) as two independent variables, with β and γ as the slope coefficients and ln(A) as the intercept, making it suitable for multiple linear regression. Among the options, \"Multiple regression\" (H) directly matches this scenario, as it involves regressing one dependent variable on multiple independents, while alternatives like single regression (one independent), logistic (binary outcomes), or regularization methods (e.g., Ridge, Lasso) do not apply to this standard linearized estimation.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 275,
        "output_tokens": 274,
        "total_tokens": 807
      },
      "provider": "azure",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.000192,
    "accuracy": 1.0
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_135",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Traders in major financial institutions use the Black-Scholes formula in a backward fashion to infer other traders' estimation of $\\sigma$ from option prices. In fact, traders frequently quote sigmas to each other, rather than prices, to arrange trades. Suppose a call option on a stock that pays no dividend for 6 months has a strike price of $35, a premium of $2.15, and time to maturity of 7 weeks. The current short-term T-bill rate is 7%, and the price of the underlying stock is $36.12. What is the implied volatility of the underlying security?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.275\nB. 0.225\nC. 0.165\nD. 0.195\nE. 0.210\nF. 0.350\nG. 0.300\nH. 0.180\nI. 0.320\nJ. 0.251\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**J**\n\nThe Black-Scholes implied volatility is calculated by solving for $\\sigma$ such that the model call price matches the observed premium of $2.15. Using $S=36.12$, $K=35$, $T=7/52 \\approx 0.1346$ years, $r=0.07$, testing shows $\\sigma \\approx 0.251$ yields a call price of approximately $2.15$, while lower values like 0.225 give $\\sim 2.02$ and higher like 0.275 give $\\sim 2.25$. Thus, option J is the closest match.\n\n\\boxed{J}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning involves solving the Black-Scholes equation inversely for implied volatility σ, where the call price formula C = S N(d1) - K e^{-rT} N(d2) must equal the observed premium of $2.15, with parameters S = 36.12, K = 35, T = 7/52 ≈ 0.1346 years, and r = 0.07. First, compute key values: ln(S/K) ≈ 0.03155, √T ≈ 0.3669, rT ≈ 0.00942, and e^{-rT} ≈ 0.9906, so K e^{-rT} ≈ 34.672. Since no closed-form solution exists for σ, evaluate the options numerically: for σ = 0.251, d1 ≈ [0.03155 + (0.07 + 0.251²/2) × 0.1346] / (0.251 × 0.3669) ≈ 0.4908, d2 ≈ 0.3987, N(d1) ≈ 0.688, N(d2) ≈ 0.655, yielding C ≈ 36.12 × 0.688 - 34.672 × 0.655 ≈ 2.15. Comparisons confirm nearby options deviate: σ = 0.225 gives C ≈ 2.02 (too low), and σ = 0.275 gives C ≈ 2.25 (too high), making 0.251 the closest match among the choices.",
      "success": true,
