    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{E}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning uses the put-call duality for FX options, linking the dollar-denominated put on yen to the yen-denominated put on dollars via put-call parity and reciprocal pricing. First, compute the USD call price on yen using put-call parity: C = P + S e^{-r_f T} - K e^{-r_d T}, where S = 0.011, K = 0.008, P = 0.0005, r_d = 0.03, r_f = 0.015, and T = 4. This yields e^{-r_f T} ≈ 0.941765, e^{-r_d T} ≈ 0.886920, so S e^{-r_f T} ≈ 0.010359, K e^{-r_d T} ≈ 0.007095, and C ≈ 0.0005 + 0.003264 = 0.003764. The yen put price is then P' = C / (S K), with S K = 0.000088, giving P' ≈ 0.003764 / 0.000088 ≈ 42.77325, matching option E. This relation holds because the Garman-Kohlhagen parameters for the reciprocal option align such that P' equals the scaled call price.",
