  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_489",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A fire insurance policy with an annual premium of $40 was canceled90 days after it took effect. What was the refundto theinsured if: (a)theinsurance was canceled by the policy holder? (b)theinsurance was canceled by the carrier?\n\nOptions: \nA. $26, $30.14\nB. $30, $26.14\nC. $24, $32.14\nD. $28, $28.14\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The pro-rata refund (insurer cancellation) is $40 × (365-90)/365 ≈ $30.14.  \nThe short-rate refund (policyholder cancellation), per standard fire insurance tables, is $26.  \nThus, option A is correct.\n\n\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by calculating the pro-rata refund for insurer cancellation, using a 365-day year: earned premium for 90 days is $40 × (90/365) ≈ $9.86, so refund is $40 - $9.86 ≈ $30.14.  \nFor policyholder cancellation, standard fire insurance short-rate tables specify retaining approximately 35% of the annual premium for 90 days, or $14 earned, resulting in a refund of $40 - $14 = $26.  \nOptions are evaluated: A matches ($26 for policyholder, $30.14 for insurer); B reverses the refunds; C implies incorrect earned amounts; D shows nearly equal refunds, inconsistent with short-rate penalties.",
      "success": true,
