    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**F**\n\nThe bond's current yield is $62.50 / $980 ≈ 6.38% (6.4% to nearest 0.1%).  \nThe preferred stock's yield is $60 / $1,010 ≈ 5.94% (5.9% to nearest 0.1%).  \nThe bond yields 0.5% more.\n\n\\boxed{F}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by identifying the annual income and purchase price for each investment to calculate current yields, as no maturity is specified for the bond.  \n\nFor the bond: annual coupon is 6.25% of $1,000 = $62.50, purchase price is 98% of $1,000 = $980, so yield = $62.50 / $980 ≈ 6.3775%, which rounds to 6.4% to the nearest 0.1% (since the hundredths digit 7 ≥ 5, round up 6.37 to 6.4).  \n\nFor the preferred stock: annual dividend per share is 6% of $100 = $6, total for 10 shares = $60, total purchase price = 10 × $101 = $1,010, so yield = $60 / $1,010 ≈ 5.9406%, which rounds to 5.9% to the nearest 0.1% (thousandths digit 0 < 5, so 5.94 remains 5.9).  \n\nThe difference is 6.4% - 5.9% = 0.5%, matching option F as the bond provides the greater effective yield by that amount.",
