    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6329",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is meant when it is said that \"the accelerator is asymmetric \"?\n\nOptions: \nA. The accelerator inversely affects the level of investment and capital creation\nB. The accelerator is only operational during periods of economic boom\nC. The acceleration principle applies only when the required capital for a certain level of output is greater than the available capital, but does not apply in the reverse situation\nD. The accelerator is a term used to describe the fluctuating interest rates on capital loans\nE. The acceleration principle applies only when the available capital for a certain level of output is greater than the required capital, but does not apply in the reverse situation\nF. The accelerator affects only the service sector but not the manufacturing sector\nG. The accelerator applies equally to capital surplus and deficit\nH. The accelerator only applies when required capital is less than available capital\nI. The accelerator applies to the rate of technological innovation in capital goods\nJ. The accelerator refers to the rate of capital depreciation\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**C**\n\nThe accelerator principle posits that investment rises proportionally with increases in output (when required capital exceeds available capital, prompting net investment), but it is asymmetric because firms do not disinvest proportionally during output declines (when available capital exceeds required, leading to excess capacity rather than capital liquidation). Option C precisely captures this one-sided application. Other options misrepresent the concept or describe unrelated ideas.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by defining the accelerator principle in economics: investment levels are proportional to changes in output, with firms increasing capital during output growth to meet demand. Asymmetry arises because this effect is stronger during expansions—when required capital exceeds available capital, triggering net investment—but weaker during contractions, as firms rarely disinvest proportionally due to excess capacity and the difficulty of reducing fixed capital. Among the options, C accurately reflects this by stating the principle applies only when required capital exceeds available capital, not vice versa, while other choices either invert the logic (e.g., E, H), describe unrelated factors (e.g., D, I, J), or imply symmetry or sector-specific limits (e.g., G, F).",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 252,
        "output_tokens": 228,
        "total_tokens": 842
      },
      "provider": "azure",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.0001644,
    "accuracy": 1
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5839",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is meant by forward and backward shifting?\n\nOptions: \nA. Forward shifting is a company increasing its product prices, backward shifting is decreasing them\nB. Forward shifting is the process of innovation and product development, while backward shifting is the phasing out of obsolete products.\nC. Forward shifting is when a government increases regulation on businesses, and backward shifting is when it decreases regulation.\nD. Forward shifting involves a company moving towards newer markets, while backward shifting means retreating from existing markets.\nE. Forward shifting occurs when a businessman is able to pass the burden of that tax onto its customers in the form of higher prices. Backward shifting occurs when this businessman is able to pass the burden of that tax onto its workers, in the form of lower wages, or onto its suppliers, in the form of lower resource payments.\nF. Forward shifting is when a company moves its operations to a more advanced technological stage, backward shifting is reverting to less advanced technology.\nG. Forward shifting relates to future business predictions while backward shifting is analyzing past business performance\nH. Forward shifting is a business expanding while backward shifting is a business downsizing\nI. Forward shifting refers to the transfer of production processes to future dates, and backward shifting refers to moving production to earlier dates.\nJ. Forward shifting is the increase in the value of a currency, while backward shifting is the decrease in the value of a currency.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**E**\n\nForward and backward shifting are standard terms in economics referring to tax incidence, where forward shifting passes the tax burden to consumers via higher prices, and backward shifting passes it to suppliers or workers via lower payments. Option E precisely matches this definition. Other options describe unrelated business or economic concepts.\n\n\\boxed{E}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe thought process begins by identifying the context of the term \"forward and backward shifting,\" which is a concept from economics, specifically tax incidence theory. It then evaluates the options systematically: Option E directly aligns with the standard definition, describing forward shifting as passing a tax burden to customers through higher prices and backward shifting as passing it to workers or suppliers via lower wages or payments. The remaining options (A–D, F–J) are dismissed as they pertain to unrelated areas like pricing strategies, innovation, regulation, market movements, technology, predictions, expansion, production timing, or currency values, none of which match the precise economic terminology. This confirms E as the most accurate choice based on established definitions.",
