    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5889",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Why did theinstitutionalistsof the early twentieth century reject laissez-faire?\n\nOptions: \nA. They held the view that government intervention would reduce economic freedom.\nB. They were convinced that laissez-faire would ensure equitable wealth distribution.\nC. They were disturbed by the wide spread poverty and the fluctuations of the business cycle.\nD. They thought laissez-faire would lead to more efficient and ethical business practices.\nE. They favored the principles of socialism.\nF. They believed in the laws of supply and demand.\nG. They were satisfied with the state of the economy.\nH. They believed laissez-faire policies would prevent economic crises.\nI. They felt that laissez-faire would naturally eliminate corruption and monopolies.\nJ. They were proponents of complete government control over the economy.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**C**\n\nInstitutional economists like Veblen, Commons, and Mitchell rejected laissez-faire due to its failure to address real-world issues such as widespread poverty and business cycle instability observed in the early 20th century. They advocated for government intervention to mitigate these problems rather than relying on unregulated markets. Other options misrepresent their views, which emphasized empirical reform over ideology.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by identifying the core concern of early 20th-century institutionalists, who critiqued laissez-faire economics based on observed economic failures. Option C directly aligns with this, as historical evidence shows they were troubled by widespread poverty during the Progressive Era and business cycle fluctuations, prompting calls for intervention. Other options are evaluated and dismissed: A, D, F, H, and I contradict their advocacy for regulation; B, G, and J inaccurately portray their stance against free markets or full socialism; and E overlooks their focus on pragmatic reforms rather than ideological socialism. This step-by-step analysis confirms C as the most accurate choice based on institutionalist principles.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 207,
        "output_tokens": 213,
        "total_tokens": 656
      },
      "provider": "azure",
