    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6225",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There is a strong demand for welders in California but Bill an unemployed welder lives in New York. Bill is\n\nOptions: \nA. frictionally unemployed.\nB. voluntarily unemployed.\nC. considered to be a hidden worker.\nD. underemployed.\nE. structurally unemployed.\nF. considered to be a discouraged worker.\nG. chronically unemployed.\nH. cyclically unemployed.\nI. seasonally unemployed.\nJ. casually unemployed.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**E**\n\nBill's skills match available jobs, but the geographic mismatch (New York vs. California) defines structural unemployment. This differs from frictional (short-term job transitions) or cyclical (economic downturns). Other options like discouraged or underemployed do not fit the scenario.\n\n\\boxed{E}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe thought process begins by identifying key elements of the scenario: Bill is an unemployed welder in New York, while strong demand exists in California, indicating a skills match but a geographic mismatch. This aligns with structural unemployment, which occurs due to mismatches in location or skills relative to job availability. Other options are ruled out as follows: frictional involves temporary transitions between jobs in the same area; cyclical relates to economic downturns; discouraged workers have stopped job searching; underemployed are partially employed; and the rest (e.g., seasonal, voluntary) do not match the described situation. Thus, option E is the most accurate based on standard economic definitions.",
