      "generated_answer": "**E**\n\nIn the circular flow model, households supply (or furnish) labor as a factor of production to businesses, which demand it to produce goods and services. Option E accurately captures this relationship, noting labor's scarcity. Other options reverse the flow or mischaracterize labor's role.\n\n\\boxed{E}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe circular flow model depicts households supplying factors of production, including labor, to businesses, which demand these inputs to create goods and services in exchange for wages. Among the options, E correctly identifies labor as a scarce resource furnished (supplied) by households and demanded by businesses, aligning with standard economic principles. Other choices inaccurately reverse this flow, misclassify labor's nature, or introduce irrelevant elements like exports or government provision.",
