    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5762",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: John Maynard Keynes actively supported direct government intervention in the economy. In particular, he advocated deficit spending as a means to end the Great Depression because he thought business was not investing enough, even with low interest rates to maintain national income and employment at their pre-Depression levels. Why did busi-ness , in the view of Keynes, fail to invest in response to the low interest rates?\n\nOptions: \nA. Businesses were waiting for further reduction in interest rates\nB. Low demand during the Depression made investments unprofitable, regardless of low interest rates.\nC. The government had imposed too many regulations, which discouraged investment\nD. Businesses lacked confidence in the economy\nE. Interest rates were not truly low; there was a misperception about the monetary policy\nF. There was a surplus of goods already, and no need for further production\nG. Businesses were facing internal management issues\nH. Businesses were primarily investing in foreign markets instead of the domestic economy\nI. Businesses were unable to secure loans due to strict banking policies\nJ. High inflation rates were diminishing the real value of potential returns on investments\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nLow demand during the Depression reduced expected profitability of investments (marginal efficiency of capital), making them unviable even at low interest rates, per Keynesian theory. Option D (lack of confidence) is related but secondary to the core demand deficiency. Other options contradict Keynes's focus on aggregate demand.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by identifying the core of Keynesian theory: businesses fail to invest despite low interest rates because investment decisions hinge on expected profitability, which depends on aggregate demand rather than just borrowing costs. Among the options, B directly aligns with this by stating that low demand during the Depression rendered investments unprofitable, regardless of interest rates, as it addresses the low marginal efficiency of capital. Options like D (lack of confidence) are connected via \"animal spirits\" but are secondary to demand deficiency, while A, C, E–J introduce unrelated factors such as regulations, inflation, or banking issues that do not reflect Keynes's primary argument. Thus, B is the most precise match based on the theoretical context.",
