    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5724",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: For a given level of government spending the federal government usually experiences a budget____during economic____and a budget ______during economic_______.\n\nOptions: \nA. surplus expansion surplus expansion\nB. deficit recession deficit expansion\nC. surplus expansion deficit recession\nD. deficit expansion deficit recession\nE. surplus expansion surplus recession\nF. deficit recession surplus expansion\nG. surplus recession deficit expansion\nH. surplus recession surplus expansion\nI. deficit expansion surplus recession\nJ. surplus recession deficit recession\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**F**\n\nThe federal budget tends to show automatic deficits during recessions (as tax revenues fall with lower incomes) and surpluses during expansions (as tax revenues rise), assuming fixed government spending. This matches option F exactly. Option C reverses the order but conveys the same idea.\n\n\\boxed{F}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by analyzing the question, which concerns automatic stabilizers in fiscal policy for fixed government spending. During economic expansions, higher incomes lead to increased tax revenues, resulting in a budget surplus; during recessions, lower incomes reduce tax revenues, causing a budget deficit. This establishes the pattern: deficit during recession and surplus during expansion. Comparing options, F (\"deficit recession surplus expansion\") directly matches this sequence, while C (\"surplus expansion deficit recession\") reverses it, though both describe the same economic dynamic. F is selected as the most likely correct answer due to its alignment with conventional phrasing in economics, such as \"deficits during recessions and surpluses during expansions,\" commonly found in textbooks.",
