    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6367",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following creates the trade-offdepicted by the Phillips curve?\n\nOptions: \nA. A decrease in input costs that corresponds with a decrease in unemployment\nB. A decrease in output that corresponds with an increase in the price level\nC. An increase in input costs that corresponds with an increase in unemployment\nD. A rightward shift in the aggregate demand curve\nE. An increase in output that corresponds with a decrease in the price level\nF. A decrease in input costs that corresponds with an increase in output\nG. An increase in output that corresponds with a decrease in unemployment\nH. A leftward shift in the aggregate supply curve\nI. A leftward shift in the aggregate demand curve\nJ. A rightward shift in the aggregate supply curve\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Phillips curve depicts a short-run trade-off between lower unemployment (higher output) and higher inflation, arising from a rightward shift in aggregate demand that moves along the upward-sloping short-run aggregate supply curve.  \nOption D matches this precisely.  \nOther options describe supply shocks or direct correlations without the inflation-unemployment trade-off.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by identifying the Phillips curve as depicting an inverse short-run relationship between inflation and unemployment, stemming from movements along the short-run aggregate supply (SRAS) curve.  \nA rightward shift in aggregate demand (option D) increases output—lowering unemployment—while raising the price level and thus inflation, directly creating this trade-off.  \nOther options fail to produce the specific inverse relationship: supply shifts (e.g., H, J) cause correlated changes in inflation and unemployment, while partial descriptions (e.g., G) omit inflation.",
      "success": true,
