    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5695",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Federal Reserve's most important control instrument is open-market operations. How is it that selling government bonds can reduce bank reserves?\n\nOptions: \nA. The bank will lose an equivalent amount of reserve balances with the Federal Reserve\nB. The bank's reserve balances will become negative as a result of the bond sale\nC. The bank's reserve balances increase due to interest payments on bonds\nD. The bank will lose reserve balances only if the bonds are sold to the public, not to the Federal Reserve\nE. The bank's reserve requirement ratio will decrease\nF. The bank will gain more reserve balances\nG. The bank's reserve balances will decrease by half the amount of the bonds sold\nH. The bank's reserve balances will double\nI. The bank's lending capacity will increase without any change in reserve balances\nJ. The bank's reserve balances remain unchanged\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**A**\n\nWhen the Federal Reserve sells government bonds in open-market operations, buyers (typically banks or the public) pay by transferring funds from their reserve accounts at the Fed, directly reducing bank reserves by an equivalent amount. This drains liquidity from the banking system to contract the money supply. Other options either contradict this mechanism or describe unrelated effects.\n\n\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by understanding open-market operations, where the Federal Reserve sells government bonds to reduce bank reserves and contract the money supply. When the Fed sells bonds, buyers—such as banks or the public—pay using their reserve balances at the Federal Reserve, causing an equivalent reduction in those balances, which aligns directly with option A. Other options are eliminated as follows: B, G, and H misstate the reserve change magnitude or direction; C, E, F, I, and J describe incorrect or opposite effects; and D inaccurately limits the scenario to public sales only.",
