      "generated_answer": "**E**\n\nWhen the price of pears increases, the law of demand predicts that the quantity demanded of pears will fall, as consumers buy less at a higher price. This is a movement along the demand curve, ceteris paribus. Option I is also true per the law of supply, but E is the most directly emphasized response in standard economics questions.\n\n\\boxed{E}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided answer, E, aligns with the law of demand, which states that an increase in the price of a good leads to a decrease in the quantity demanded, assuming other factors remain constant. This represents a movement along the existing demand curve for pears. Among the options, E (\"Quantity demanded of pears falls\") is the most likely correct choice, as it directly reflects the standard introductory economics principle emphasized in such questions, distinguishing it from supply-side effects like I or incorrect demand shifts like H and J.",
