    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5989",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Education makes Chris a better worker, voter, parent, and citizen. Because the benefits from education go beyond those that Chris enjoys himself, education provides\n\nOptions: \nA. no externalities and should not be subsidized\nB. decreasing marginal utility and should be taxed\nC. increasing marginal utility and should be subsidized\nD. increasing marginal utility and should not be subsidized\nE. externalities and should be taxed\nF. externalities and should not be subsidized\nG. externalities and should be subsidized\nH. increasing marginal utility and should be taxed\nI. no externalities and should be subsidized\nJ. decreasing marginal utility and should not be taxed\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**G**\n\nEducation provides positive externalities because its benefits extend to society (e.g., better voters and citizens), beyond the individual. Positive externalities justify government subsidies to increase consumption to the socially optimal level.  \n\\boxed{G}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by identifying the core issue: the statement indicates that education's benefits extend beyond the individual (e.g., improving society as a worker, voter, parent, and citizen), which defines positive externalities. Positive externalities occur when social benefits exceed private benefits, leading to underproduction in the market. To correct this market failure and achieve the socially optimal level, subsidies are recommended to encourage greater provision. Among the options, G (\"externalities and should be subsidized\") directly matches this analysis, as other choices either misidentify the issue (e.g., no externalities or marginal utility) or suggest incorrect policies (e.g., taxing for positive externalities).",
