    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5966",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: To close an expansionary gap\n\nOptions: \nA. the aggregate demand curve should not be shifted.\nB. the aggregate demand curve should be shifted to the right.\nC. the aggregate supply curve should be shifted to the left.\nD. both the aggregate demand and supply curves should not be shifted.\nE. the aggregate supply curve should be shifted to the right, and the aggregate demand curve to the left.\nF. the aggregate supply curve should be shifted to the right.\nG. the aggregate demand curve should be shifted to the left.\nH. both the aggregate demand and supply curves should be shifted to the left.\nI. the aggregate supply curve should not be shifted.\nJ. both the aggregate demand and supply curves should be shifted to the right.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**G**\n\nAn expansionary (inflationary) gap occurs when aggregate demand exceeds potential output, causing inflation. To close it, contractionary policy shifts the AD curve left to reduce demand and restore equilibrium at potential output. Other options either do nothing, worsen the gap, or address supply-side issues unnecessarily.\n\n\\boxed{G}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by defining an expansionary gap as a situation where actual output exceeds potential output, indicating that aggregate demand is too high relative to full-employment aggregate supply. To close this gap, the focus is on reducing aggregate demand through contractionary fiscal or monetary policy, which shifts the aggregate demand curve to the left, thereby lowering output and price levels back to equilibrium. Among the options, G directly matches this by specifying a leftward shift in the aggregate demand curve, while alternatives like shifting aggregate supply or not shifting curves fail to address the demand-side imbalance or could exacerbate the problem.",
