    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5873",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which one of the following would be a plausible response to a finding of residual non-normality?\n\nOptions: \nA. Use a logarithmic functional form instead of a linear one\nB. Add lags of the variables on the right hand side of the regression model\nC. Estimate the model in first differenced form\nD. Remove any large outliers from the data.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "D. Removing large outliers from the data can directly address residual non-normality, as outliers often cause skewness or heavy tails in the residual distribution. Transformations like logarithms (A) may help in cases of skewness but are less universally targeted at non-normality than outlier removal. Options B and C address autocorrelation and non-stationarity, respectively, not primarily non-normality.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided reasoning systematically evaluates the options against standard regression diagnostics for residual non-normality, a violation of OLS assumptions. Residual non-normality often stems from outliers, skewness, or model misspecification, with option D (removing large outliers) directly targeting a primary cause, as outliers induce skewness or fat tails in residuals. Option A (logarithmic transformation) addresses skewness from functional form issues but is less universally applicable; B (adding lags) remedies autocorrelation, and C (first differencing) handles non-stationarity, neither of which primarily fixes non-normality. Thus, D emerges as the most plausible and direct response based on common econometric practices.",
