    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5709",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If XYZ Corporation buys an original Matisse painting to hang in its board room then\n\nOptions: \nA. GDP increases because the painting is considered a luxury good.\nB. GDP is unaffected because it is a second hand sale.\nC. GDP is unaffected because the painting is considered a non-monetary asset.\nD. GI)P decreases because I decreases.\nE. GDP is unaffected because the painting is considered a depreciating asset.\nF. GDP increases because it is considered an investment in art.\nG. GDP decreases by the amount of the purchase because C decreases.\nH. GDP decreases because the painting is considered an unproductive asset.\nI. GDP decreases by the amount of the purchase because G decreases.\nJ. GDP increases by the amount of the purchase because I increases.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nBuying an existing original Matisse painting is a second-hand transaction of a previously produced asset, which does not represent new economic production and thus leaves GDP unaffected. GDP measures the value of newly produced final goods and services, excluding resales of used items. Options suggesting changes via C, I, or G misapply GDP components to non-new production.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by identifying GDP as a measure of the value of newly produced goods and services in an economy, excluding transactions involving existing assets. For the scenario where XYZ Corporation buys an original Matisse painting—a pre-existing artwork created decades ago—the purchase qualifies as a second-hand sale, similar to buying a used car, which does not add to current production and leaves GDP unchanged. Options like B directly align with this principle, while alternatives such as F or J incorrectly treat it as new investment (I), and others misapply components like C or G without basis in national accounts rules.",
