      "generated_answer": "**J**\n\n\\( R^2 = 0 \\) means SSE = SST, so all fitted values \\(\\hat{y}_i = \\bar{y}\\) (a constant), implying all slope coefficients are zero (i), the fitted line is horizontal/flat with respect to explanatory variables (ii), and no variability in \\( y \\) is explained (iii). The intercept is \\(\\bar{y}\\), not necessarily zero (iv false).\n\n\\boxed{J}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by recalling that \\( R^2 = 0 \\) indicates SSE equals SST, meaning the fitted values \\(\\hat{y}_i\\) are all identical to the mean \\(\\bar{y}\\), so the model explains no variation in \\( y \\). This directly confirms statement (iii), as the regression line accounts for none of the variability of \\( y \\) about its mean. For the fitted values to be constant across observations, the slope coefficients must be zero in OLS regression, verifying (i), and the fitted line must be horizontal (constant) with respect to all explanatory variables, confirming (ii). Statement (iv) is false because the intercept equals \\(\\bar{y}\\), which is not necessarily zero. Thus, only (i), (ii), and (iii) hold true, corresponding to option J.",
