    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5663",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which one of the following factors is likely to lead to a relatively high degree of out-of-sample forecast accuracy?\n\nOptions: \nA. A model that contains many variables\nB. A model that is based on financial theory\nC. A model whose dependent variable has recently exhibited a structural change\nD. A model that heavily relies on subjective judgment\nE. A model that does not consider economic indicators\nF. A model that is entirely statistical in nature with no room for judgmental modification of forecasts\nG. A model that is based on a single variable\nH. A model that predicts based on historical trends only\nI. A model with a high degree of complexity\nJ. A model that ignores market volatility\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nA model based on financial theory is likely to capture underlying economic relationships, improving generalization to out-of-sample data compared to purely data-driven or ad-hoc approaches. Options like A, I (complexity), G (oversimplification), and others risk overfitting, underfitting, or ignoring key dynamics. Theory provides a robust foundation less prone to spurious correlations seen in statistical-only models (F, H).\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by defining out-of-sample forecast accuracy as the model's performance on unseen data, emphasizing generalization over fitting to training data. It then evaluates each option: A, I (many variables or high complexity) risk overfitting; C (structural change) undermines data relevance; D (subjective judgment), E (no economic indicators), F (purely statistical without judgment), G (single variable), H (historical trends only), and J (ignores volatility) all introduce biases, oversimplification, or missed factors that harm out-of-sample performance. Option B stands out because financial theory identifies causal economic mechanisms, reducing reliance on spurious patterns and enhancing predictive reliability on new data.",
