  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6051",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which one of the following is a disadvantage of the general to specific or \"LSE\" (\"Hendry\") approach to building econometric models, relative to the specific to general approach?\n\nOptions: \nA. Some variables may be excluded at the first stage leading to coefficient biases\nB. The final model may lack theoretical interpretation\nC. The final model may be statistically inadequate\nD. If the initial model is mis-specified, all subsequent steps will be invalid.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nThe general-to-specific (Hendry) approach starts with a highly general model and sequentially simplifies it via tests, but if this initial model omits key variables or is otherwise misspecified, all subsequent reductions inherit those errors, invalidating the process. In contrast, the specific-to-general approach begins simply and builds up, potentially detecting and correcting omissions iteratively. Options A and C describe risks more aligned with specific-to-general starts, while B is not a primary critique.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process evaluates the general-to-specific (Hendry) approach, which begins with a comprehensive model and applies sequential tests to eliminate insignificant variables, against the specific-to-general alternative.\n\nIt first identifies the core disadvantage: the method's reliance on a correctly specified initial general model, as any misspecification propagates through all simplification steps, matching option D.\n\nOptions are then assessed sequentially: A applies more to specific-to-general by risking early exclusions; B overlooks the approach's aim for theory-consistent outcomes; and C contradicts its emphasis on testing for statistical adequacy at each stage.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 146,
        "output_tokens": 215,
