  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6402",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Marx prided himself on his 'scientific' analysis of capital-ism. Through this analysis, he felt he had proved how the problemsfacing the nineteenth century were intimately re-latedto the capitalist mode of production. Modern radical economistshold the similar view that the problems of modernWestern society are inherent in capitalist production. Give the orthodox rebuttal to this viewpoint.\n\nOptions: \nA. Elimination of capitalism can resolve all societal problems.\nB. Many non-capitalist societies suffer from the same problems as Western, capitalist countries.\nC. The capitalist mode of production is the only system that has consistently reduced poverty worldwide.\nD. Problems in Western society are solely due to individual moral failures, not the economic system.\nE. Capitalism naturally corrects any societal problems through market forces without the need for intervention.\nF. Non-Western capitalist societies do not encounter any social or economic problems.\nG. Capitalism is the sole cause of problems in modern Western society.\nH. The problems of modern Western society are a result of technological advancement, not the capitalist production mode.\nI. Only capitalist societies face issues such as exploitation, pollution, and economic inequality.\nJ. Capitalist societies have the best track record for solving social and economic problems.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nThe orthodox rebuttal argues that societal problems (e.g., inequality, poverty) are not unique or inherent to capitalism, as they persist in non-capitalist systems. Option B directly counters the Marxist claim by highlighting this universality. Other options either support the radical view, overstate capitalism's virtues, or misattribute causes.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by identifying the core claim: problems in Western society are inherent to capitalist production, as per Marx and modern radical economists. The orthodox rebuttal must demonstrate that these issues are not exclusive to capitalism. Option B achieves this by noting that many non-capitalist societies experience the same problems, directly undermining the idea of inherent causality. Other options fail: A and G support the radical view; C, E, and J praise capitalism without refuting inherency; D, F, H, and I introduce unrelated or inaccurate attributions. Thus, B provides the most precise and logical counterargument.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 297,
        "output_tokens": 193,
        "total_tokens": 914
      },
