  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_813",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Was the use of armed force permitted prior to the United Nations Charter?\n\nOptions: \nA. Armed force was only permitted in self-defense\nB. Armed force was permitted with many restrictions\nC. Armed force was not regulated under international law prior to 1945\nD. Armed force was permitted but required approval from an international body\nE. Armed force was prohibited\nF. Armed force was permitted but required approval from the countries involved\nG. Armed force was permitted subject to few restrictions\nH. Armed force was permitted with no restrictions\nI. Armed force was permitted with the consent of all nations\nJ. Armed force was not permitted under any circumstances\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**H**\n\nPrior to the 1945 UN Charter, international law imposed no general prohibition on the use of armed force (jus ad bellum), allowing states broad discretion to resort to war without legal restrictions on the decision itself, though rules on wartime conduct (jus in bello) existed. Instruments like the League of Nations Covenant and Kellogg-Briand Pact introduced limited norms but lacked enforcement and did not ban force outright. Option H best captures this historical reality.\n\n\\boxed{H}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by identifying the historical context: the UN Charter of 1945 marked the first comprehensive prohibition on the use of force under Article 2(4), so the question concerns international law beforehand. Classical international law, including customary norms before the 20th century, treated the resort to armed force (jus ad bellum) as a sovereign right, with no binding general restrictions, though rules on wartime conduct (jus in bello) applied. Instruments like the League of Nations Covenant (1919) and Kellogg-Briand Pact (1928) imposed limited procedural delays or declarations against aggressive war but were ineffective, non-universal, and did not prohibit force outright, as confirmed by sources such as Shaw's International Law. Options were evaluated systematically: A, E, and J reflect post-Charter rules; B, D, F, and I imply non-existent requirements; C overlooks jus in bello regulations; G understates the lack of meaningful jus ad bellum limits; thus, H—armed force was permitted with no restrictions—accurately describes the pre-1945 era.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 188,
        "output_tokens": 319,
        "total_tokens": 1679
      },
      "provider": "azure",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.0001971,
    "accuracy": 0.0
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1246",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A wedding planner owned a summer cottage on the lake. In order to facilitate the access to the cottage, which is located on a knoll above the lake, the wedding planner entered into an agreement with a neighbor, an adjoining land owner, whereby the neighbor, in writing, granted the wedding planner a right-of-way over a strip of land 30 feet in width and a quarter of a mile in length along the eastern margin of the neighbor's property. Without notif'ing the neighbor, the wedding planner proceeded with his plan to improve the roadbed by having the road asphalted in order to make it more accessible for motor vehicle traffic. Several years later, the neighbor started a sand business, which required him to do heavy hauling that subsequently destroyed the asphalted surface on the road. Ten years after the neighbor started his sand business, the wedding planner sold his lakefront property to an artist. Shortly after the artist took possession of the cottage and property, the neighbor erected wooden barriers across the roadway, thus obstructing the access to the artist's property. The interest that the wedding planner acquired in the access road may best be described as\n\nOptions: \nA. an easement by estoppel.\nB. an easement of light and air.\nC. a prescriptive easement.\nD. an express easement.\nE. a profit a prendre.\nF. an easement by necessity.\nG. an easement implied by prior use.\nH. a license.\nI. an easement of support.\nJ. an easement in gross.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nThe written grant of a right-of-way over the neighbor's land explicitly created an express easement, as it was a formal agreement conveying a specific property interest for access to the cottage. This easement is appurtenant, benefiting the dominant estate (cottage property) and transferable upon sale to the artist. Other options like license (revocable permission), prescriptive (adverse use), or necessity (strict unity/severance requirements) do not fit the facts of a clear written grant.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 355,
        "output_tokens": 103,
        "total_tokens": 876
      },
      "provider": "azure",
