    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1242",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which statement best describes the profit sharing relationship of a general partnership where the partners have agreed only on voting percentage and the voting shares are unequal?\n\nOptions: \nA. Partners share in proportion to their experience and expertise.\nB. Partners share in proportion to their contributions to the capital and assets of the partnership.\nC. Partners share in proportion to their voting percentage.\nD. Partners cannot share until they unanimously agree upon a distribution.\nE. Partners share in proportion to their role in the partnership.\nF. Partners share according to a predetermined ratio agreed upon at the start of the partnership.\nG. Partners share in proportion to the time they spend working for the partnership.\nH. Partners share is determined by a third-party arbitrator.\nI. Partners share equally.\nJ. Partners share in proportion to their individual profits.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In a general partnership, absent an agreement on profit sharing, profits are shared equally by default under the Uniform Partnership Act, regardless of unequal voting percentages or other factors. The agreement on voting only affects management decisions, not profit distribution. Thus, option I is correct.\n\n\\boxed{I}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided answer is based on the default rules of general partnerships under the Uniform Partnership Act (UPA), Section 401(b), which mandates equal sharing of profits and losses unless the partnership agreement specifies otherwise. Here, the partners agreed only on unequal voting percentages, which govern management decisions but do not alter profit-sharing arrangements. Therefore, profit sharing defaults to equal shares among partners, matching option I and eliminating others like C (proportional to voting) or B (proportional to contributions), as no such modifications were made.",
