      "generated_answer": "**H**\n\nIn many jurisdictions handling first-degree murder (often capital) cases, statutes and rules of criminal procedure (e.g., in states like Florida, Georgia, and North Carolina) require that a list of prospective jurors' names and addresses be furnished to the defendant upon request, typically before voir dire begins. This right is not conditioned on prosecutorial agreement, good cause, court order, payment, or judicial discretion, distinguishing it from options like A, C, D, G, I, and J. It is not automatic immediately after charging (B), nor withheld entirely (E, F).\n\n\\boxed{H}",
