    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1103",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the meaning of \"armed attack\" in Article 51 UN Charter?\n\nOptions: \nA. Armed attack includes all types of armed force\nB. Armed attack includes all high intensity instances of armed force\nC. Armed attack includes terrorist attacks\nD. An \"armed attack\" gives the right to invade the aggressor State\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nArticle 51 of the UN Charter permits self-defense only in response to an \"armed attack,\" which, per ICJ jurisprudence (e.g., *Nicaragua v. United States*), requires armed force of sufficient scale and effects, not all instances. Option A is too broad, C remains debated for non-state actors, and D misstates proportionality limits. Thus, B best captures the \"high intensity\" threshold.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by referencing Article 51 of the UN Charter, which allows self-defense only if an \"armed attack\" occurs, without defining the term explicitly. It then draws on ICJ jurisprudence, particularly the *Nicaragua v. United States* case, establishing that an \"armed attack\" requires armed force of sufficient scale and effects, excluding minor or low-level uses of force. Options are evaluated as follows: A is overly broad by including all armed force; C is uncertain due to debates over non-state actors like terrorists; D incorrectly implies automatic invasion rights, ignoring necessity and proportionality; thus, B aligns most closely with the \"high intensity\" threshold from legal precedent.",
