    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1680",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A march was held to call attention to the fact that minorities are still disenfranchised from the electoral process. A congressional report revealed that in the certain parts of the country, only 42% of minorities eligible to vote were, in fact, registered. The report also indicated that certain states had residency laws restricting a person's right to vote. As a consequence, Congress enacted a statute that makes any law denying minorities the right to vote shall be deemed unconstitutional. This federal statute will most likely be upheld under which of the following constitutional provisions?\n\nOptions: \nA. Twenty-sixth Amendment.\nB. Tenth Amendment.\nC. Eighth Amendment.\nD. Seventeenth Amendment.\nE. Twentieth Amendment.\nF. Fourteenth Amendment.\nG. Thirteenth Amendment.\nH. Nineteenth Amendment.\nI. First Amendment.\nJ. Fifteenth Amendment.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**J**\n\nThe Fifteenth Amendment prohibits denying the right to vote based on race, color, or previous condition of servitude, and Congress has enforcement authority under its second section, directly supporting the statute's aim to invalidate discriminatory voting laws. Other options, like the Fourteenth Amendment (equal protection) or Nineteenth Amendment (women's suffrage), do not specifically address racial disenfranchisement in voting. The statute aligns with landmark legislation like the Voting Rights Act, upheld under the Fifteenth Amendment.\n\n\\boxed{J}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by analyzing the statute's purpose: it declares unconstitutional any law denying minorities the right to vote, which directly addresses racial disenfranchisement as highlighted in the context of low registration rates and restrictive state laws. This aligns with the Fifteenth Amendment, which explicitly prohibits denying or abridging voting rights based on race, color, or previous condition of servitude, and grants Congress enforcement power under its second section. Other options are ruled out as follows: Fourteenth Amendment covers equal protection but not voting rights specifically by race; Nineteenth Amendment addresses women's suffrage; Twenty-sixth Amendment lowers the voting age; and the rest (Tenth, Eighth, Seventeenth, Twentieth, Thirteenth, First) pertain to unrelated matters like states' rights, punishment, senatorial elections, presidential terms, slavery, or speech. Thus, the Fifteenth Amendment provides the constitutional basis most likely to uphold the statute, consistent with precedents like the Voting Rights Act.",
