    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1088",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A widget manufacturer and a widget retailer signed the following agreement:\"The manufacturer promises to sell and the retailer promises to buy 10,000 widgets at $50 per 100. One thousand widgets are to be delivered by the manufacturer on the first day of each of the next 10 months. EACH MONTHLY DELIVERY ISA SEPARATE CONTRACT. Payment to be made within 10 days of receipt of each shipment. \"The agreement between the manufacturer and the retailer may best be interpreted as\n\nOptions: \nA. a bilateral contract.\nB. a periodic contract.\nC. 10 separate contracts.\nD. a contract of adhesion.\nE. a conditional contract.\nF. a requirements-output contract.\nG. a divisible contract.\nH. a unilateral contract.\nI. a void contract.\nJ. an installment contract.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The agreement specifies that \"EACH MONTHLY DELIVERY IS A SEPARATE CONTRACT,\" which is classic language creating a divisible contract where each performance (delivery and payment) is independent and enforceable separately. Although structured as one agreement for 10,000 widgets in monthly installments, the clause apportions it into divisible parts rather than literally 10 independent contracts or merely an installment contract. Thus, it is best interpreted as a divisible contract.\n\n\\boxed{G}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe agreement's explicit clause—\"EACH MONTHLY DELIVERY IS A SEPARATE CONTRACT\"—indicates that the overall contract is structured to treat each monthly delivery and corresponding payment as independent obligations, aligning with the legal concept of a divisible contract. In contract law, a divisible contract divides performance into separable parts where a breach of one part does not necessarily affect the others, as supported by standard interpretations in sources like Williston on Contracts. Among the options, this distinguishes it from alternatives like C (10 separate contracts, which implies fully independent agreements rather than one divided contract), J (installment contract, a broader category without emphasizing divisibility), and others that are either too general (e.g., A, bilateral) or inapplicable (e.g., F, requirements-output).",
