    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The investor's designation of the 10-acre tract for a public school in the recorded subdivision plan, promoted to benefit purchasers of the adjacent lots, creates a burden on that tract enforceable by those lot owners under a common subdivision scheme. This is best described as an equitable servitude, as it imposes a use restriction for the benefit of the neighboring parcels without a formal easement or covenant deed. Other options like easements or covenants do not fit, as this arises from the plat and sales context rather than possession, liens, zoning, or invalidity.\n\n\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided AI-generated answer identifies option A (equitable servitude) as correct based on the following logical steps derived from property law principles. The investor's recorded subdivision plan designates the 10-acre tract for public school use while promoting the subdivision to buyers, creating a common scheme where purchasers of the 90 lots rely on this designation for their benefit. This imposes a use restriction on the 10-acre tract, enforceable in equity by those lot owners against the investor, characteristic of an equitable servitude in subdivision contexts. Other options are eliminated as follows: easements (B, D, I) involve non-possessory use rights unsuitable for school construction requiring fee ownership; restrictive covenants (F) typically denote express private agreements rather than plat-based schemes; and invalid or unenforceable restrictions (C, H), liens (G), adverse possession (E), or zoning (J) do not align with the scenario's promotional and recorded plat elements.",
