    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1822",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Congress has recently enacted a statute requiring all males between the ages of 18 and 30 to take a physical examination each year. The results of the exam are sent to a government data information center for the purpose of keeping information about men who may be drafted into the military. A 25-year-old law school graduate has herpes. He has recently sent resumes to many governmental agencies. Fearful that the information about his herpes condition will become available, he seeks a declaratory judgment that would forbid the government from requiring him to take a physical examination. Which of the following is the best constitutional basis in support of the federal law?\n\nOptions: \nA. The full faith and credit clause.\nB. The dormant commerce clause.\nC. The necessary and proper clause.\nD. The equal protection clause.\nE. The commerce clause.\nF. The supremacy clause.\nG. To raise and support an army and navy.\nH. The privileges and immunities clause.\nI. To provide for the general welfare.\nJ. The due process clause.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**G**\n\nThe federal law prepares data for potential military draft purposes, directly invoking Congress's enumerated power under Article I, Section 8 to \"raise and support Armies\" (and maintain a Navy). Other options like the Commerce Clause or Necessary and Proper Clause are less directly applicable, as this is not commerce regulation or merely auxiliary to another power. Equal protection or due process would more likely support challenges to the law's gender/age distinctions, not its validity.\n\n\\boxed{G}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by analyzing the statute's purpose: maintaining data on males aged 18-30 for potential military draft, which aligns with Congress's enumerated powers under Article I, Section 8. Among the options, \"To raise and support an army and navy\" (G) provides the most direct constitutional basis, as it explicitly authorizes Congress to prepare for military needs, including draft-related measures like the Selective Service Act. Other options are dismissed because: A, B, F, and H relate to interstate or state-federal relations; C is supplementary to enumerated powers; D and J address individual rights challenges rather than affirmative authority; E involves commerce regulation; and I pertains to spending, none of which core to military readiness. Thus, G is the strongest support for the law's validity.",
