    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1530",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the relationship between the formal sources of international law?\n\nOptions: \nA. Treaties supersede custom\nB. Treaties and General Principles of Law are of equal importance, but both supersede custom\nC. General Principles of Law supersede both custom and treaties\nD. Judicial decisions and teachings of the most highly qualified publicists are the only sources of international law.\nE. Judicial decisions and teachings of the most highly qualified publicists supersede both custom and treaties\nF. Custom and General Principles of Law are of equal importance, but both supersede treaties\nG. There is a hierarchy, but it varies from case to case\nH. Custom supersedes treaties\nI. Treaties and custom are of equal importance, but General Principles of Law supersede both\nJ. There is no hierarchy between the formal sources of international law\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**J**\n\nArticle 38(1) of the ICJ Statute lists treaties, custom, and general principles as primary (\"formal\") sources of international law without establishing a hierarchy among them; they are of equal status and applied based on relevance. Judicial decisions and scholarly writings (option D/E) are subsidiary means under Article 38(1)(d), not formal sources. Options asserting supremacy (e.g., A, C, H) contradict this non-hierarchical structure.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
