  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_831",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Because of a sudden and unanticipated severe shortage of heating fuel, the President has ordered all offices of federal executive agencies to be open only four days per week. The President's order allows an exception to the extent that emergency circumstances require different hours of operation (as in the case of federal hospitals). When Congress enacted the appropriations statute for operating all federal executive agencies, its members assumed that the offices of those agencies would be open five days per week, but Congress did not include such a requirement in its appropriations statute or in any other statute. Is the President's order constitutional?\n\nOptions: \nA. No, because the heads of the various executive agencies have final responsibility for the operation of those agencies' offices.\nB. No, because when they passed the statute appropriating monies for the operation of executive agencies, members of Congress assumed that those agencies' offices would be open five days per week.\nC. Yes, because the Constitution vests the President with plenary authority to direct the administration of all federal agencies in any manner the President deems expedient.\nD. Yes, because the order relates to the management of the executive branch and is not prohibited by any statute.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nThe President's order manages executive branch operations amid a crisis and imposes no statutory violation, as Congress's appropriations did not mandate five-day operations. Agency heads serve under presidential authority (contra A), and congressional assumptions lack legal force (contra B). While presidential power is broad, \"plenary\" overstates it (contra C).\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by identifying the core issue: whether the President's order to limit federal executive agency offices to four days per week due to a fuel shortage violates any constitutional or statutory limits. Congress appropriated funds without specifying operational days, leaving management discretion to the executive branch. Option A fails because agency heads operate under presidential direction, not independently. Option B is invalid as congressional assumptions do not create binding law—only explicit statutes do, and none exists here. Option C inaccurately claims \"plenary\" authority, which overstates limits on presidential power, whereas Option D correctly aligns with the President's inherent authority over executive branch management when no statute prohibits it. Thus, D is the most accurate choice based on separation of powers principles.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 282,
        "output_tokens": 218,
        "total_tokens": 741
      },
      "provider": "azure",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.0001654,
    "accuracy": 0.0
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1665",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An American company operates as an importer and distributor of guitars manufactured by a foreign company, and maintains several warehouses throughout the U. S. for the wholesale distribution of the guitars. A warehouse located in a city handles the distribution of guitars for several state areas. The guitars and guitar strings are shipped separately to the city warehouse. The guitar strings are mingled, stacked, and stored in the warehouse along with various other guitar strings. The guitars, on the other hand, arrive completely assembled in their shipping crates and remain on the loading docks. The city imposes an ad valorem property tax on the guitars and strings. The power of the city to impose an ad valorem tax on the foreign company's guitars and/or strings would most likely be upheld under\n\nOptions: \nA. the necessary and proper clause.\nB. the contract clause.\nC. the import and export clause.\nD. the equal protection clause.\nE. the supremacy clause.\nF. the commerce clause.\nG. the due process clause.\nH. the reserved power of the states as granted under the Tenth Amendment.\nI. the establishment clause.\nJ. the privileges and immunities clause.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
