    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_810",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which statement below best represents Durkheim's view of the function of punishment?\n\nOptions: \nA. Vengeance.\nB. Deterrence.\nC. Restoration.\nD. Moral education.\nE. Indemnification.\nF. Rehabilitation.\nG. Social control.\nH. Incapacitation.\nI. Desert.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Durkheim viewed punishment as a mechanism to reaffirm the collective conscience and shared moral norms, thereby educating society and strengthening social solidarity. This aligns most closely with \"moral education\" among the options.  \n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe thought process begins by identifying Durkheim's sociological perspective on punishment, as outlined in works like \"The Division of Labor in Society,\" where it functions to reaffirm collective sentiments and moral boundaries rather than individual retribution or practical deterrence. Options are then evaluated: A (vengeance) reflects personal motives; B (deterrence) aligns with utilitarian views like Bentham's; C (restoration), E (indemnification), F (rehabilitation), H (incapacitation), and I (desert) pertain to other justice paradigms; G (social control) is too vague. This leaves D (moral education) as the best match, as it captures punishment's role in educating society about norms and reinforcing solidarity.",
