    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{F}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe scenario involves a contract for the sale of goods (bread), governed by UCC Article 2. UCC § 2-209(2) states that a signed agreement requiring modifications to be in writing is enforceable, preventing oral modifications. Here, the contract explicitly required modifications in writing on a signed separate sheet, making the oral agreement on May 1 unenforceable. Thus, option F correctly identifies that the modifying contract was not in writing and therefore unenforceable under the UCC, while options like H incorrectly apply common law consideration rules irrelevant to UCC modifications.",
