      "generated_answer": "The second equation simplifies to \\(\\log_b n = \\frac{1}{b-1}\\).\n\nThe first equation simplifies to \\(\\sqrt{\\log_b n} = \\frac{1}{2} \\log_b n\\).\n\nLet \\(x = \\log_b n > 0\\), so \\(\\sqrt{x} = \\frac{1}{2} x\\), which gives \\(x = 4\\).\n\nThus, \\(\\frac{1}{b-1} = 4\\), so \\(b = \\frac{5}{4}\\) and \\(n = \\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)^4 = \\frac{625}{256}\\).\n\nThen \\(j + k = 625 + 256 = 881\\).\n\n\\boxed{881}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by simplifying the second equation. Substituting \\(x = \\log_b n\\) yields \\(b x = 1 + x\\), so \\(x(b - 1) = 1\\) and \\(x = \\frac{1}{b-1}\\).\n\nNext, the first equation simplifies using logarithm properties: \\(\\log_b \\sqrt{n} = \\frac{1}{2} \\log_b n = \\frac{1}{2} x\\), and \\(\\sqrt{\\log_b n} = \\sqrt{x}\\), giving \\(\\sqrt{x} = \\frac{1}{2} x\\).\n\nSolving \\(\\sqrt{x} = \\frac{1}{2} x\\) for \\(x > 0\\): let \\(y = \\sqrt{x}\\) (so \\(y > 0\\)), then \\(y = \\frac{1}{2} y^2\\), or \\(\\frac{1}{2} y^2 - y = 0\\), so \\(y \\left( \\frac{1}{2} y - 1 \\right) = 0\\). Thus, \\(y = 2\\) (discarding \\(y = 0\\) as it leads to \\(x = 0\\), which fails the second equation), so \\(x = 4\\).\n\nEquating expressions for \\(x\\): \\(\\frac{1}{b-1} = 4\\), so \\(b - 1 = \\frac{1}{4}\\) and \\(b = \\frac{5}{4}\\). Then \\(n = b^4 = \\left( \\frac{5}{4} \\right)^4 = \\frac{625}{256}\\), where 625 and 256 are coprime.\n\nFinally, \\(j = 625\\), \\(k = 256\\), so \\(j + k = 881\\).",
