    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The product simplifies to $\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k^2-1)\\log_k 5}{(k^2-4)\\log_{k+1} 5} = \\left[ \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k-1)(k+1)}{(k-2)(k+2)} \\right] \\cdot \\frac{\\log_4 5}{\\log_{64} 5}$.  \nThe first product telescopes to $\\frac{62}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{65} = 31 \\cdot \\frac{1}{13} = \\frac{31}{13}$, and $\\frac{\\log_4 5}{\\log_{64} 5} = \\log_4 64 = 3$, so the product is $\\frac{31}{13} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{93}{13}$.  \nThus, $m = 93$, $n = 13$, and $m + n = 106$.\n\n\\boxed{106}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided answer derives from simplifying the product $\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\log_k (5^{k^2-1})}{\\log_{k+1} (5^{k^2-4})}$, which equals $\\left[ \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k^2-1}{k^2-4} \\right] \\cdot \\left[ \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\log_k 5}{\\log_{k+1} 5} \\right]$.  \nThe logarithmic product telescopes: $\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\log_k 5}{\\log_{k+1} 5} = \\frac{\\log_4 5}{\\log_{64} 5} = \\log_4 64 = 3$, since $4^3 = 64$.  \nThe coefficient product factors as $\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k-1}{k-2} \\cdot \\frac{k+1}{k+2}$, where the first factor telescopes to $\\frac{62}{2} = 31$ and the second to $\\frac{5}{65} = \\frac{1}{13}$, yielding $\\frac{31}{13} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{93}{13}$, so $m=93$, $n=13$, and $m+n=106$.",
