    "global index": "AIME_67",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There exist real numbers $x$ and $y$, both greater than 1, such that $\\log_x\\left(y^x\\right)=\\log_y\\left(x^{{4y}}\\right)=10$. Find $xy$.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Given \\(\\log_x(y^x) = 10\\), it follows that \\(x \\log_x y = 10\\), so \\(\\log_x y = 10/x\\).\n\nGiven \\(\\log_y(x^{4y}) = 10\\), it follows that \\(4y \\log_y x = 10\\), so \\(\\log_y x = 5/(2y)\\).\n\nSince \\(\\log_y x = 1 / \\log_x y\\), substitute to get \\(5/(2y) = x/10\\), so \\(xy = 25\\).\n\nSuch \\(x, y > 1\\) exist satisfying the equations.\n\n\\boxed{25}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by simplifying the given logarithmic equations. From \\(\\log_x(y^x) = 10\\), apply the power rule to obtain \\(x \\log_x y = 10\\), so \\(\\log_x y = 10/x\\). From \\(\\log_y(x^{4y}) = 10\\), apply the power rule to get \\(4y \\log_y x = 10\\), so \\(\\log_y x = 5/(2y)\\).\n\nNext, use the reciprocal property of logarithms: \\(\\log_y x = 1 / \\log_x y\\). Substitute the expressions: \\(5/(2y) = 1 / (10/x) = x/10\\). Cross-multiplying yields \\(5 \\cdot 10 = 2y \\cdot x\\), simplifying to \\(50 = 2xy\\), so \\(xy = 25\\).\n\nThis relation ensures consistency between the equations. To confirm existence, note that substituting \\(y = 25/x\\) into \\(\\log_x y = 10/x\\) leads to the equation \\(x^{1 + 10/x} = 25\\), which has solutions for \\(x > 1\\) and \\(x < 25\\) (since \\(y > 1\\)), as the function \\(x^{1 + 10/x}\\) is continuous, starts below 25 near \\(x = 1+\\), and exceeds 25 for \\(x > 1.5\\). Thus, real numbers \\(x, y > 1\\) exist satisfying both original equations with \\(xy = 25\\).",
