    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There are $ n $ values of $ x $ in the interval $ 0 < x < 2\\pi $ where $ f(x) = \\sin(7\\pi \\cdot \\sin(5x)) = 0 $. For $ t $ of these $ n $ values of $ x $, the graph of $ y = f(x) $ is tangent to the $ x $-axis. Find $ n + t $.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The equation \\(\\sin(7\\pi \\sin(5x)) = 0\\) implies \\(\\sin(5x) = k/7\\) for integers \\(k\\) with \\(|k| \\leq 7\\). Let \\(\\theta = 5x \\in (0, 10\\pi)\\). There are 10 solutions for each of the 12 values \\(|k| = 1\\) to \\(6\\), 9 solutions for \\(k=0\\), and 5 solutions each for \\(k = \\pm 7\\), so \\(n = 12 \\times 10 + 9 + 10 = 139\\). The graph is tangent to the x-axis where \\(f(x) = 0\\) and \\(f'(x) = 0\\), which occurs precisely at the 10 roots for \\(k = \\pm 7\\), so \\(t = 10\\). Thus, \\(n + t = 149\\).\n\n\\boxed{{149}}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by solving \\(f(x) = \\sin(7\\pi \\sin(5x)) = 0\\), which occurs when \\(7\\pi \\sin(5x) = k\\pi\\) for integer \\(k\\), or \\(\\sin(5x) = k/7\\) with \\(|k| \\leq 7\\). Substituting \\(\\theta = 5x \\in (0, 10\\pi)\\), the number of solutions for \\(\\sin(\\theta) = m\\) where \\(m = k/7\\) is determined: 10 solutions for each of the 12 values \\(|k| = 1\\) to \\(6\\) (two per period over 5 periods), 9 solutions for \\(k=0\\) (at \\(\\theta = \\pi, 2\\pi, \\dots, 9\\pi\\)), and 5 solutions each for \\(k = \\pm 7\\) (\\(m = \\pm 1\\)), yielding \\(n = 12 \\times 10 + 9 + 10 = 139\\).\n\nNext, tangency requires \\(f(x_0) = 0\\) and \\(f'(x_0) = 0\\). Differentiating gives \\(f'(x) = 35\\pi \\cos(7\\pi \\sin(5x)) \\cos(5x)\\). At roots, \\(\\cos(7\\pi \\sin(5x)) = \\cos(k\\pi) = \\pm 1 \\neq 0\\), so \\(f'(x_0) = 0\\) iff \\(\\cos(5x_0) = 0\\), or \\(5x_0 = \\pi/2 + m\\pi\\), where \\(\\sin(5x_0) = (-1)^m = \\pm 1\\). These correspond exactly to the roots for \\(k = \\pm 7\\), of which there are 10, so \\(t = 10\\).\n\nThus, \\(n + t = 139 + 10 = 149\\).",
