    "global index": "AIME_89",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There is a collection of $25$ indistinguishable white chips and $25$ indistinguishable black chips. Find the number of ways to place some of these chips in the $25$ unit cells of a $5\\times5$ grid such that: \n\neach cell contains at most one chip\nall chips in the same row and all chips in the same column have the same colour\nany additional chip placed on the grid would violate one or more of the previous two conditions.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A maximal placement requires filling complete rectangular subgrids of matching colors such that every row and column has at least one chip, and no further chip can be added without violating conditions. This occurs precisely when partitioning rows into white set of size $a$ and black set of size $5-a$, columns into white set of size $b$ and black of size $5-b$, filling the $a \\times b$ white subgrid and $(5-a) \\times (5-b)$ black subgrid completely, for either $(a,b)=(0,0)$, $(5,5)$, or $1 \\leq a,b \\leq 4$. The number of ways is $2 + \\sum_{a=1}^4 \\sum_{b=1}^4 \\binom{5}{a} \\binom{5}{b} = 2 + 30^2 = 902$.\n\n\\boxed{{902}}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning identifies maximal placements as those where chips fully occupy rectangular subgrids defined by partitions of rows and columns into white and black sets, ensuring every row and column contains at least one chip and no empty cell allows an additional chip. Specifically, for row white set size $a$ and column white set size $b$, chips fill the $a \\times b$ white subgrid and $(5-a) \\times (5-b)$ black subgrid, valid only for $(a,b) = (0,0)$ (all black), $(5,5)$ (all white), or $1 \\leq a,b \\leq 4$ (mixed cases ensuring all rows/columns assigned). The total count is the two monochromatic cases plus the mixed cases: $2 + \\left( \\sum_{a=1}^4 \\binom{5}{a} \\right)^2 = 2 + (5 + 10 + 10 + 5)^2 = 2 + 30^2 = 2 + 900 = 902$.",
