    "global index": "AIME_112",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: From an unlimited supply of 1-cent coins, 10-cent coins, and 25-cent coins, Silas wants to find a collection of coins that has a total value of $ N $ cents, where $ N $ is a positive integer. He uses the so-called **greedy algorithm**, successively choosing the coin of greatest value that does not cause the value of his collection to exceed $ N $. For example, to get 42 cents, Silas will choose a 25-cent coin, then a 10-cent coin, then 7 1-cent coins. However, this collection of 9 coins uses more coins than necessary to get a total of 42 cents; indeed, choosing 4 10-cent coins and 2 1-cent coins achieves the same total value with only 6 coins.\n\nIn general, the greedy algorithm succeeds for a given $ N $ if no other collection of 1-cent, 10-cent, and 25-cent coins gives a total value of $ N $ cents using strictly fewer coins than the collection given by the greedy algorithm. Find the number of values of $ N $ between 1 and 1000 inclusive for which the greedy algorithm succeeds.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The greedy algorithm succeeds for all $N$ where $N \\mod 25 \\notin \\{5,6,7,8,9,15,16,17,18,19\\}$ (15 such residues, totaling 600 values) and additionally for $N=5,6,7,8,9,15,16,17,18,19$ (10 values), for a total of 610 values.\n\n\\boxed{{610}}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning identifies the residues \\( r = N \\mod 25 \\) where the greedy algorithm fails for \\( q = \\lfloor N/25 \\rfloor \\geq 1 \\). For each \\( r \\) from 0 to 24, compute greedy coins \\( gc(r) = \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor + (r \\mod 10) \\). Greedy fails for a given \\( N = 25q + r \\) if there exists \\( k \\geq 1 \\) with \\( k \\leq q \\) such that coins for \\( r + 25k \\) using 10-cent and 1-cent coins is strictly less than \\( k + gc(r) \\).\n\nCalculations show this holds only for \\( k=1 \\) and specifically for \\( r \\in \\{5,6,7,8,9,15,16,17,18,19\\} \\) (10 values), as \\( \\text{coins10_1}(r+25) < 1 + gc(r) \\) for these, and no other \\( k \\) or \\( r \\) satisfies the condition. Thus, for these \"bad\" \\( r \\), greedy succeeds only when \\( q=0 \\) (i.e., \\( N = r \\), 10 cases). For the remaining 15 \"good\" \\( r \\) (0-4, 10-14, 20-24), no \\( k \\) beats greedy, so it succeeds for all such \\( N \\).\n\nFrom 1 to 1000, there are exactly 40 values for each \\( r \\): 40 for \\( r=0 \\) and 40 each for \\( r=1 \\) to 24. The good residues contribute \\( 15 \\times 40 = 600 \\) successes. Adding the 10 cases for bad \\( r \\) with \\( q=0 \\) gives 610 total successes.",
