    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Place the circumcenter $O$ at $(0,0)$ and $I$ at $(\\sqrt{13},0)$, using $d^2 = R(R-2r) = 13$. Vertex $A$ is at $(\\sqrt{13}, 2\\sqrt{39})$. Then $\\sin(A/2) = \\sqrt{39}/13$ and $\\cos(A/2) = \\sqrt{130}/13$. The directions from $A$ along $AB$ and $AC$ are $(\\sin(A/2), -\\cos(A/2))$ and $(-\\sin(A/2), -\\cos(A/2))$. The product $AB \\cdot AC = 4(h^2 \\cos^2(A/2) - d^2 \\sin^2(A/2)) = 4(120 - 3) = 468$, where $h = 2\\sqrt{39}$.\n\n\\boxed{468}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by placing the circumcenter O at (0,0) and the incenter I at (√13, 0), derived from Euler's distance formula d² = R(R - 2r) = 13(13 - 12) = 13. Vertex A is determined by the perpendicularity condition IA ⊥ OI, yielding A at (√13, √(169 - 13)) = (√13, 2√39). The distance AI = 2√39 equals r / sin(A/2) = 6 / sin(A/2), so sin(A/2) = √39 / 13 and cos(A/2) = √130 / 13.\n\nThe angle bisector from A is vertical along x = √13, so the directions of AB and AC are obtained by rotating the unit vector (0, -1) by ±A/2, giving vectors (sin(A/2), -cos(A/2)) and (-sin(A/2), -cos(A/2)). For a line from A in unit direction v intersecting the circumcircle, the distance to the other intersection is 2 |A · v|.\n\nThus, AB · AC = 4 |(A · v1)(A · v2)|, where v1 = (sin(A/2), -cos(A/2)) and v2 = (-sin(A/2), -cos(A/2)). Compute A · v1 = √13 sin(A/2) - 2√39 cos(A/2) and A · v2 = -√13 sin(A/2) - 2√39 cos(A/2), so (A · v1)(A · v2) = [2√39 cos(A/2)]² - [√13 sin(A/2)]² = (2√39)² (130/169) - 13 (39/169) = 156(130/169) - 507/169 = 120 - 3 = 117. Therefore, AB · AC = 4 × 117 = 468.",
