      "generated_answer": "The largest prime \\(p < 1000\\) satisfying the conditions is 349, achieved with \\(z = 18 + 5i\\) (or equivalent up to signs and swap), where \\(\\operatorname{Re}(z^3) = 4482\\), \\(\\operatorname{Im}(z^3) = 4735\\), and the side lengths 349, 4482, 4735 satisfy the triangle inequalities.\n\n\\boxed{349}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning identifies primes \\(p < 1000\\) that are sums of two squares, as required by \\(|z| = \\sqrt{p}\\) with integer real and imaginary parts \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), so \\(p = a^2 + b^2\\). For \\(z = a + bi\\), compute \\(z^3\\) to get \\(\\operatorname{Re}(z^3) = a(a^2 - 3b^2)\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Im}(z^3) = b(3a^2 - b^2)\\), yielding side lengths \\(p\\), \\(x = |a(a^2 - 3b^2)|\\), and \\(y = |b(3a^2 - b^2)|\\), which must satisfy the triangle inequalities, primarily \\(|x - y| < p\\) since \\(x + y > p\\) and the other inequalities follow when this holds.\n\nPrimes congruent to 1 mod 4 or 2 are candidates, checked from largest to smallest. The condition \\(|x - y| < p\\) requires \\(a/b\\) near \\(\\tan(\\pi/12) \\approx 0.2679\\) (or reciprocal \\(\\approx 3.732\\)) or near 1, minimizing the angular difference for \\(z^3\\). Computations show larger primes like 997 (\\(31^2 + 6^2\\)), 761 (\\(20^2 + 19^2\\)), and 613 (\\(18^2 + 17^2\\)) fail as \\(|x - y|\\) exceeds \\(p\\) (e.g., 9361 > 997, 2281 > 761).\n\nFor \\(p = 349 = 18^2 + 5^2\\) (\\(a=18\\), \\(b=5\\)), \\(x = 18 \\times 249 = 4482\\), \\(y = 5 \\times 947 = 4735\\), so \\(|x - y| = 253 < 349\\), satisfying inequalities (e.g., \\(4482 + 349 = 4831 > 4735\\)). No larger prime meets the condition, confirming 349 as the largest.\n\n<explanation_end",
