    "global index": "GeoBench_1311",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following identifies the novel feature that allowed early automobile industrial workers high enough wages that they could afford the products that they were making?\n\nOptions: \nA. The outsourcing of low-paying jobs\nB. The long hours employees spent in poor working conditions\nC. The specialization of individual jobs\nD. The beginning of multinational companies\nE. The congregation of innovators in one area\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**C**\n\nThe novel feature was the specialization of individual jobs (division of labor on the assembly line), which dramatically increased productivity in early automobile manufacturing, enabling Henry Ford to pay high wages (e.g., $5/day) while keeping car prices affordable for workers. This Fordist system contrasted with prior low-wage models. Other options like outsourcing or long hours were not the key enablers.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by identifying the historical context of early automobile manufacturing, particularly Henry Ford's innovations, where high wages enabled workers to afford the products they produced. It then evaluates each option against this context: Option A (outsourcing) is dismissed as a modern practice not relevant to early auto industry; Option B (long hours in poor conditions) was common but did not drive wage increases; Option D (multinational companies) did not apply to Ford's initial domestic operations; and Option E (congregation of innovators) contributed to industry growth but was not the direct mechanism for higher wages. Option C (specialization of individual jobs) is selected as it directly aligns with the assembly line's division of labor, which boosted productivity, reduced costs, and allowed for sustainable high wages under Fordism. This step-by-step elimination confirms C as the most accurate answer based on economic history.",
