    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nThe sector model, developed by Homer Hoyt, explains urban social structure through wedge-shaped sectors extending outward from the central business district (CBD) along transportation routes, rather than circular zones based solely on distance. This contrasts with the concentric zone model (option C), which emphasizes distance-based socioeconomic gradients. Options A, B, and E misrepresent the model by implying isolation, peripheral highway focus, or CBD independence.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided answer identifies option D as correct by first recalling that the sector model, proposed by Homer Hoyt, describes urban land use as wedge-shaped sectors radiating from the central business district (CBD) along transportation corridors, influencing socioeconomic patterns. It then evaluates each option: A is incorrect as the model does not involve zoning or gating; B partially relates to transportation but inaccurately frames growth as primarily peripheral; C aligns with the concentric zone model, not sectors; and E contradicts the CBD-centric nature of the model. Thus, D accurately captures the emphasis on transportation systems over mere distance from the CBD as the key organizing factor.",
