      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nBid-rent theory posits that land values decrease from the central business district (CBD) outward, as different users bid rents based on location benefits; commercial activities (e.g., offices, retail) generate the highest bids for centrality and thus dominate the expensive CBD. Wealthier households prefer cheaper peripheral land for space, contradicting option A. Other options (C-E) do not address the core rent gradient or urban land use patterns.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by recalling that bid-rent theory describes urban land use patterns where users bid for locations based on economic benefits, resulting in the highest rents in the central business district (CBD) for activities that gain most from centrality. Option B aligns with this, as commercial users outbid others for CBD land due to proximity advantages, making it the only option directly explaining the theory. Options A, C, D, and E are dismissed because A incorrectly places wealthier families in the CBD (they suburbanize for space), C pertains to retail competition unrelated to land bidding, D misrepresents urban housing characteristics, and E addresses renting costs without linking to the rent gradient.",
