    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The general term simplifies to \\(\\frac{F_{n+1}}{F_n F_{n+2}} = \\frac{1}{F_n} - \\frac{1}{F_{n+2}}\\) using the Fibonacci recurrence \\(F_{n+2} = F_{n+1} + F_n\\).  \nThe sum \\(\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{F_n} - \\frac{1}{F_{n+2}} \\right)\\) is telescoping, with partial sum \\(S_N = 1 + 1 - \\frac{1}{F_{N+1}} - \\frac{1}{F_{N+2}}\\).  \nAs \\(N \\to \\infty\\), \\(S_N \\to 2\\).\n\n\\boxed{2}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by simplifying the general term \\(\\frac{F_{n+1}}{F_n F_{n+2}}\\) using the Fibonacci recurrence \\(F_{n+2} = F_{n+1} + F_n\\). This yields \\(\\frac{F_{n+1}}{F_n F_{n+2}} = \\frac{1}{F_n} - \\frac{1}{F_{n+2}}\\), as derived from the identity \\(\\frac{1}{F_n F_{n+1}} - \\frac{1}{F_{n+1} F_{n+2}} = \\frac{1}{F_n F_{n+2}}\\) multiplied by \\(F_{n+1}\\).  \n\nThe infinite sum then becomes \\(\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{F_n} - \\frac{1}{F_{n+2}} \\right)\\), a telescoping series. The partial sum up to \\(N\\) is \\(S_N = \\frac{1}{F_1} + \\frac{1}{F_2} - \\frac{1}{F_{N+1}} - \\frac{1}{F_{N+2}}\\), where intermediate terms cancel.  \n\nSince \\(F_1 = 1\\) and \\(F_2 = 1\\), \\(S_N = 2 - \\frac{1}{F_{N+1}} - \\frac{1}{F_{N+2}}\\). As \\(N \\to \\infty\\), \\(F_{N+1}\\) and \\(F_{N+2}\\) grow without bound, so the sum converges to 2.",
