    "global index": "MATH_1",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem and provide the final answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Define\n\\[p = \\sum_{{k = 1}}^\\infty \\frac{{1}}{{k^2}} \\quad \\text{{and}} \\quad q = \\sum_{{k = 1}}^\\infty \\frac{{1}}{{k^3}}.\\]Find a way to write\n\\[\\sum_{{j = 1}}^\\infty \\sum_{{k = 1}}^\\infty \\frac{{1}}{{(j + k)^3}}\\]in terms of $p$ and $q.$\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The double sum \\(\\sum_{j=1}^\\infty \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j+k)^3}\\) can be rewritten by setting \\(m = j + k\\), so for each \\(m \\geq 2\\), there are \\(m-1\\) pairs \\((j,k)\\) with \\(j,k \\geq 1\\). This yields \\(\\sum_{m=2}^\\infty (m-1) \\frac{1}{m^3} = \\sum_{m=2}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{m^2} - \\frac{1}{m^3} \\right) = (p - 1) - (q - 1) = p - q\\).\n\n\\boxed{p - q}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by reindexing the double sum \\(\\sum_{j=1}^\\infty \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j+k)^3}\\) using the substitution \\(m = j + k\\), where \\(m\\) ranges from 2 to infinity. For each fixed \\(m \\geq 2\\), the pairs \\((j, k)\\) with \\(j, k \\geq 1\\) and \\(j + k = m\\) number exactly \\(m-1\\), leading to \\(\\sum_{m=2}^\\infty (m-1) \\frac{1}{m^3}\\). Simplifying the term gives \\(\\frac{m-1}{m^3} = \\frac{1}{m^2} - \\frac{1}{m^3}\\), so the sum becomes \\(\\sum_{m=2}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{m^2} - \\frac{1}{m^3} \\right) = \\left( \\sum_{m=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{m^2} \\right) - \\left( \\sum_{m=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{m^3} \\right)\\). These partial sums relate to the full series as \\(\\sum_{m=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{m^2} = p - 1\\) and \\(\\sum_{m=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{m^3} = q - 1\\), yielding \\((p - 1) - (q - 1) = p - q\\).",
