    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The ellipse \\(kx^2 + y^2 = 1\\) with \\(k > 1\\) has semi-axes \\(a = 1/\\sqrt{k}\\) along \\(x\\) and \\(b = 1\\) along \\(y\\), foci at \\((0, \\pm c)\\) where \\(c = \\sqrt{1 - 1/k}\\), and \\(x\\)-intercepts at \\((\\pm a, 0)\\) where the tangents are vertical lines \\(x = \\pm a\\).\n\nA circle tangent to the ellipse at both \\((\\pm a, 0)\\) must have vertical tangents there, so its center is at \\((h, 0)\\) with radius \\(|h - a| = |h + a|\\), which implies \\(h = 0\\) and radius \\(a\\). Thus, the circle is \\(x^2 + y^2 = a^2 = 1/k\\).\n\nFor it to pass through the foci, \\(c = a\\), so \\(\\sqrt{1 - 1/k} = 1/\\sqrt{k}\\), hence \\(1 - 1/k = 1/k\\), \\(1 = 2/k\\), and \\(k = 2\\).\n\n\\boxed{2}",
