    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**F**\n\nTransgressive segregation refers to the phenomenon in the F₂ generation (or later filial generations) where genetic recombination produces individuals with phenotypes that exceed the range of either parent, often due to the additive effects of alleles from both parents. This is commonly observed in quantitative traits like yield or height in breeding programs. Option F accurately captures this definition.\n\n\\boxed{F}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by recalling the genetic definition of transgressive segregation: a phenomenon where F₂ generation individuals exhibit phenotypes more extreme than either parental type due to recombination of alleles. Each option is then evaluated against this definition: A, C, D, G, H, and I are eliminated for describing uniformity, dominance, identity, reduced variability, intermediates, or equal alleles, which do not match; B and E are dismissed as they incompletely or inaccurately address the core idea of exceeding parental extremes. Option F is selected as it precisely states that F₂ variability leads to individuals showing more extreme development than parental types, aligning directly with established genetic principles. This confirms F as the most accurate choice within the given constraints.",
