    "global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3099",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The predominant cell type infected by HIV is CD4^+ cells. The mostimportant of these are the T-helper cells. (Review the immunogeneticschapter for a description of T_H-cells.) As the diseaseprogresses through latency into AIDS-related complexand finally full-blown AIDS, the absolute number of CD4^+ cells (i.e., T_H-cells) decreases.The rate of T_H-cell deletionincreases as the patient enters ARC. As the number ofT_H-cells decreases, the ratio of CD4^+ to CD8^+ cells inverts, goingfrom about 2:1 to less than 1:2. Why is the T_H-cell sucha devastating target ?\n\nOptions: \nA. T_H-cells are auxiliary cells that enhance the phagocytic function of macrophages without affecting lymphocytes.\nB. T_H-cells are necessary for the proper functioning of the specific immune system, that is, for cytotoxic T-cells (T_c) and most B-cells.\nC. T_H-cells are necessary for the functioning of only B-cells\nD. T_H-cells are primarily involved in the production of mucus as a barrier against pathogens.\nE. T_H-cells are the primary cell type that produces histamine in response to allergens.\nF. T_H-cells are necessary for the maintenance of the skin's barrier function.\nG. T_H-cells are only involved in the initial recognition of pathogens, without influencing the adaptive immune response.\nH. T_H-cells are necessary for the functioning of non-specific immune system\nI. T_H-cells are responsible for the production of antibodies against only non-pathogenic antigens.\nJ. T_H-cells are not necessary for the immune system\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nT_H-cells (CD4+ T-helper cells) are essential coordinators of the adaptive (specific) immune response, providing signals via cytokines and direct interactions to activate cytotoxic T-cells (T_c/CD8+) for killing infected cells and most B-cells for antibody production. Their depletion by HIV disrupts these functions, leading to immune collapse in AIDS. Other options misrepresent T_H-cell roles (e.g., C limits to B-cells only; A/H focus incorrectly on innate immunity).\n\n\\boxed{B}",
