    "global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3086",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following statements concerning a sarcomere of a striated muscle (such as skeletal muscle) is correct?\n\nOptions: \nA. The A band shortens during muscle contraction.\nB. During contraction H zones become elongated.\nC. The proteins troponin and tropomyosin constitute the thick and thin filaments, respectively.\nD. Myosin filaments are found in the I band.\nE. Troponin and tropomyosin are located in the H zone.\nF. Tropomyosin binds to myosin, blocking the actin-binding site.\nG. The thin filaments are composed entirely of myosin.\nH. In the relaxed position tropomyosin impedes myosin's access to the binding site of actin.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**H**\n\nIn the relaxed state, tropomyosin covers the myosin-binding sites on actin filaments, preventing cross-bridge formation and thus impeding contraction. All other options misstate sarcomere structure, filament composition, or contraction dynamics (e.g., A band length remains constant, H zone shortens, thick filaments are myosin).  \n\\boxed{H}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe thought process begins by recalling sarcomere structure: Z lines anchor thin actin filaments in I bands, while A bands contain thick myosin filaments with H zones (myosin only) at the center and M lines in the middle. During contraction, thin filaments slide over thick ones, shortening I bands and H zones while A band length stays constant.\n\nOptions are evaluated systematically: A is incorrect as A band length does not change; B is wrong since H zones shorten; C misidentifies troponin and tropomyosin, which are regulatory proteins on thin actin filaments, not the filaments themselves; D places myosin in the I band, which contains only actin; E locates troponin and tropomyosin in the H zone, but they reside on thin filaments absent there; F inaccurately states tropomyosin binds myosin, whereas it blocks actin's myosin-binding sites; G wrongly attributes myosin to thin filaments, which are actin-based.\n\nOption H is correct because, in the relaxed state, tropomyosin covers actin's myosin-binding sites, preventing cross-bridge attachment, as confirmed by sliding filament theory.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 193,
        "output_tokens": 290,
        "total_tokens": 887
      },
      "provider": "azure",
      "error": null
