      "generated_answer": "**F**\n\nSodium citrate chelates calcium ions, which are essential for the blood coagulation cascade, thereby preventing clot formation in stored blood. This is a standard anticoagulation method for blood donations. Other options do not align with citrate's primary biochemical role.\n\n\\boxed{F}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided answer derives from the context that citrate ions bind tightly to calcium, rendering it unavailable for reactions. Blood coagulation requires calcium as a cofactor in the clotting cascade, so sodium citrate inhibits this process by chelation. Among the options, only F (\"prevent coagulation\") directly matches this mechanism, while others contradict it or are unrelated.",
