    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**J**\n\nThe magnetic moment μ for ²⁰⁵Tl (I = ½) is μ = γ ℏ I, where γ = 1.569 × 10⁸ T⁻¹ s⁻¹ and ℏ ≈ 1.055 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.  \nThis yields γ ℏ ≈ 1.655 × 10⁻²⁶ J T⁻¹, so μ = (1.655 × 10⁻²⁶) × ½ = 0.827 × 10⁻²⁶ J T⁻¹, matching option J.  \nOther options result from common errors like omitting the I = ½ factor or using h instead of ℏ.\n\n\\boxed{J}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by identifying the formula for the nuclear magnetic moment: μ = γ ℏ I, where γ = 1.569 × 10⁸ T⁻¹ s⁻¹ is the gyromagnetic ratio for ²⁰⁵Tl and I = ½ is the nuclear spin.  \nNext, ℏ (reduced Planck's constant) is approximately 1.055 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, so γ ℏ is calculated as 1.569 × 10⁸ × 1.055 × 10⁻³⁴ ≈ 1.655 × 10⁻²⁶ J T⁻¹.  \nThen, μ = (1.655 × 10⁻²⁶) × ½ = 0.827 × 10⁻²⁶ J T⁻¹, which directly matches option J, while other options arise from errors such as neglecting the I factor or using incorrect constants.",
