  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_3475",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Would you expect He_2^\\oplus to be more stable than He_2? Than He?\n\nOptions: \nA. He_2^\\oplus is the most stable among He, He_2, and He_2^\\oplus\nB. He_2^\\oplus is more stable than He_2 but less stable than He\nC. He, He_2, and He_2^\\oplus have the same stability\nD. He_2^\\oplus is the least stable among He, He_2, and He_2^\\oplus\nE. He and He_2 have the same stability, both more stable than He_2^\\oplus\nF. He_2^\\oplus is more stable than He but less stable than He_2\nG. He_2^\\oplus is less stable than both He_2 and He\nH. He_2^\\oplus and He_2 have the same stability, both more stable than He\nI. He_2^\\oplus and He have the same stability, both less stable than He_2\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nHe₂ has a bond order of 0 (unstable, no covalent bond), while He₂⁺ has a bond order of 0.5 (weakly stable covalent bond), making He₂⁺ more stable than He₂. Atomic He is a stable neutral atom with no tendency to form bonds, so it is more stable than He₂⁺. Thus, He > He₂⁺ > He₂.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning evaluates stability based on molecular orbital theory and bond orders for the species involved. He₂ has four electrons, filling the σ1s bonding orbital (2 electrons) and σ*1s antibonding orbital (2 electrons), resulting in a bond order of (2-2)/2 = 0, indicating it is unstable with no covalent bond. He₂⁺ has three electrons, with two in σ1s and one in σ*1s, yielding a bond order of (2-1)/2 = 0.5, making it more stable than He₂ due to the presence of a weak covalent bond. Atomic He, as a stable noble gas atom with a filled 1s² configuration, has no bonding tendency and is inherently more stable than the molecular ion He₂⁺, leading to the order He > He₂⁺ > He₂, which matches option B.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 270,
        "output_tokens": 278,
