    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_3855",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: From the following five statements, indicate which ones are correct when an ideal gas is allowed to escape rever-sibly and isothermally into a vacuum. [Hint: What is the average kinetic energy of an ideal gas?] 1. W = 0, \\DeltaE = q = a positive number 2. w = P\\DeltaV + V\\DeltaP 3. w = P\\DeltaV, \\DeltaE = 0, q = - P\\DeltaV and is a negative number 4. \\DeltaE = 0, q = - w = a positive number 5. w = 0, q = 0, \\DeltaE = 0\n\nOptions: \nA. All statements are correct\nB. Statements 3 and 4 are correct\nC. Statements 2 and 5 are correct\nD. Statement 1 is correct\nE. Statement 2 is correct\nF. Statements 2, 4, and 5 are correct\nG. Statements 1, 3, and 5 are correct\nH. Statement 4 is correct\nI. Statement 5 is correct\nJ. Statements 1 and 2 are correct\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "This is free expansion of an ideal gas into vacuum (isothermal, \\(\\Delta E = 0\\)), where \\(P_\\text{ext} = 0\\) so \\(w = 0\\) and \\(q = 0\\). Only statement 5 is correct; others contradict this (e.g., 1 has \\(\\Delta E > 0\\), 4 implies \\(w \\neq 0\\)). The \"reversibly\" label does not change the thermodynamics, as expansion into vacuum cannot be reversible.\n\n\\boxed{I}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by identifying the process as free expansion of an ideal gas into a vacuum, which is isothermal, meaning internal energy change \\(\\Delta E = 0\\) since \\(U\\) depends only on temperature for an ideal gas. In free expansion, external pressure \\(P_\\text{ext} = 0\\), so work \\(w = -\\int P_\\text{ext} \\, dV = 0\\), and by the first law \\(\\Delta E = q + w\\), heat \\(q = 0\\). Evaluating statements: 1 is incorrect (\\(\\Delta E \\neq\\) positive); 2 is incorrect (work formula does not apply); 3 is incorrect (\\(w \\neq P\\Delta V\\)); 4 is incorrect (\\(w \\neq 0\\), so \\(q \\neq\\) positive); 5 is correct (\\(w = 0\\), \\(q = 0\\), \\(\\Delta E = 0\\)). Thus, only statement 5 holds, corresponding to option I, regardless of the \"reversibly\" descriptor, as the process inherently yields these values.",
