    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "B. 1.0\n\nThe sequence \\(X_n\\) consists of i.i.d. Exp(1) random variables. By the second Borel-Cantelli lemma, \\(P(X_n \\geq \\log n \\text{ i.o.}) = 1\\) since \\(\\sum P(X_n \\geq \\log n) = \\sum n^{-1} = \\infty\\), so \\(\\limsup X_n / \\log n \\geq 1\\) a.s. By the first Borel-Cantelli lemma, \\(P(X_n \\geq (1+\\epsilon) \\log n \\text{ i.o.}) = 0\\) for any \\(\\epsilon > 0\\) since \\(\\sum n^{-(1+\\epsilon)} < \\infty\\), so \\(\\limsup X_n / \\log n \\leq 1\\) a.s. Thus, \\(\\limsup X_n / \\log n = 1\\) with probability 1.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
