      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{E}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by taking the natural logarithm of the infinite product, yielding ln P = ∑_{n=0}^∞ x^n [ln(1 + x^{n+1}) - ln(1 + x^n)], which simplifies to a telescoping series: ln P = -ln 2 + (1 - x) ∑_{k=1}^∞ x^{k-1} ln(1 + x^k).  \n\nAs x → 1^-, this takes the indeterminate form 0 ⋅ ∞, so substitute t = 1 - x → 0^+ and approximate the sum S(x) = ∑_{k=1}^∞ x^{k-1} ln(1 + x^k) using a Riemann sum, converting it to the integral ∫_0^∞ e^{-v} ln(1 + e^{-v}) dv.  \n\nThe integral evaluates to ∑_{m=1}^∞ (-1)^{m+1} / [m(m+1)] = 2 ln 2 - 1 via series expansion and partial fractions, so lim ln P = -ln 2 + 2 ln 2 - 1 = ln 2 - 1, and thus lim P = e^{ln 2 - 1} = 2/e ≈ 0.73575888, matching option E.",
