  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7754",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Consider a horizontal strip of N+2 squares in which the first and the last square are black and the remaining N squares are all white. Choose a white square uniformly at random, choose one of its two neighbors with equal probability, and color this neighboring square black if it is not already black. Repeat this process until all the remaining white squares have only black neighbors. Let $w(N)$ be the expected number of white squares remaining. What is the limit of $w(N)/N$ as $N$ goes to infinity?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.32192809\nB. 0.4567890\nC. 0.36787944\nD. 0.22313016\nE. 0.2789456\nF. 0.5\nG. 0.13533528\nH. 0.69314718\nI. 0.5789098\nJ. 0.60653065\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by modeling the problem as a single segment of n white squares flanked by black squares, defining w(n) as the expected number of remaining white squares. For n=1, w(1)=1 since it is already isolated. For n≥2, the first effective move colors a square black, with probabilities derived from uniform selection of a white square and random direction, conditional on the move being effective.\n\nThe probabilities are: P(color end square)=1/(2n-2) each, and P(color internal k)=1/(n-1) for k=2 to n-1. This yields the recurrence: w(n) = \\frac{1}{n-1} \\left[ w(n-1) + 2 \\sum_{j=1}^{n-2} w(j) \\right], where s(n-2) = \\sum_{j=1}^{n-2} w(j).\n\nRewriting gives (n-1) w(n) + w(n-1) = 2 s(n-1). The ordinary generating function W(x) = \\sum w(n) x^n satisfies the differential equation x W'(x) + (x-1) W(x) = 2 x \\frac{W(x)}{1-x}, simplifying to x(1-x) W'(x) = (x^2 + 1) W(x). Solving yields W(x) = \\frac{x e^{-x}}{(1-x)^2}.\n\nThe asymptotic behavior follows from singularity analysis at x=1: W(x) = g(x) (1-x)^{-2} with g(x) = x e^{-x}, g(1) = 1/e, and g'(1)=0. Thus, w(n) ∼ (1/e) (n+1), so lim_{n→∞} w(n)/n = 1/e ≈ 0.36787944, matching option C.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 251,
        "output_tokens": 402,
