    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by recognizing that compass and straightedge constructions produce lengths in field extensions of the rationals with degrees that are powers of 2. For a cube with volume X times that of a given unit cube, the required edge length is the cube root of X, so √[3]{X} must lie in such an extension.\n\nNext, the values are analyzed: 3 and 17 are primes, so their cube roots have minimal polynomials t^3 - 3 and t^3 - 17, both irreducible over Q by Eisenstein's criterion, yielding degree-3 extensions. Since 3 does not divide any 2^k, √[3]{3} and √[3]{17} are not constructible.\n\nFor 8=2^3, 27=3^3, 343=7^3, and 1331=11^3, the cube roots are 2, 3, 7, and 11, respectively, which are rational integers and thus constructible. This yields the list [0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1], matching option C.",
