    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe sequence satisfies \\(x_n x_{n+1} = n\\), so \\(x_{n+1} = \\frac{n}{x_n}\\) and \\(\\frac{x_n}{x_{n+1}} = \\frac{x_n^2}{n}\\). The limit condition \\(\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{x_n}{x_{n+1}} = 1\\) implies \\(\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{x_n^2}{n} = 1\\), so \\(x_n \\sim \\sqrt{n}\\).\n\nThe recurrence every two steps gives \\(x_{n+2} = \\frac{n+1}{n} x_n\\). For odd indices, let \\(y_k = x_{2k-1}\\), then \\(y_{k+1} = \\frac{2k}{2k-1} y_k\\) with \\(y_1 = x_1\\), yielding \\(y_k = x_1 \\cdot \\frac{2^{2k-2} [(k-1)!]^2}{(2k-2)!}\\).\n\nApplying Stirling's approximation to \\(y_k\\) for large \\(k\\), where \\(n = 2k-1 \\approx 2k\\), gives \\(y_k \\sim x_1 \\sqrt{\\pi (k-1)}\\). Since \\(k \\approx n/2\\), this becomes \\(x_n \\sim x_1 \\sqrt{\\pi n / 2}\\). The limit \\(\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{x_n}{\\sqrt{n}} = 1\\) requires \\(x_1 \\sqrt{\\pi / 2} = 1\\), so \\(x_1 = \\sqrt{2 / \\pi}\\) and \\(x_1^2 = 2 / \\pi\\).\n\nThus, \\(\\pi x_1^2 = \\pi \\cdot (2 / \\pi) = 2\\).",
