      "generated_answer": "**I**\n\nThe random walk has positive drift \\(\\mu = E[X_i] = 0.6\\), so \\(\\max_{k \\leq n} S_k / n \\to 0.6\\) and \\(E[\\max_{k \\leq n} S_k]/n \\to 0.6\\) in the limit. The minimum \\(\\min_{k \\leq n} S_k\\) converges to a finite random variable \\(M_\\infty\\) almost surely with \\(E[M_\\infty] > -\\infty\\), so \\(E[\\min]/n \\to 0\\). Thus, \\(R_n = \\max - \\min + 1\\) satisfies \\(n^{-1} E[R_n] \\to 0.6\\), as the visited sites form the interval \\([\\min, \\max]\\).\n\n\\boxed{I}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by recognizing that the random walk has a positive drift \\(\\mu = E[X_i] = 0.8 \\cdot 1 + 0.2 \\cdot (-1) = 0.6\\), causing \\(S_n / n \\to \\mu\\) almost surely and \\(E[\\max_{k \\leq n} S_k] / n \\to 0.6\\). For this nearest-neighbor walk on the integers, the set of visited positions up to time \\(n\\) is the consecutive interval from \\(\\min_{k \\leq n} S_k\\) to \\(\\max_{k \\leq n} S_k\\), so \\(R_n = \\max_{k \\leq n} S_k - \\min_{k \\leq n} S_k + 1\\). Due to the positive drift, \\(\\min_{k \\leq n} S_k\\) converges almost surely to a finite random variable \\(M_\\infty\\) with finite expectation, implying \\(E[\\min_{k \\leq n} S_k] / n \\to 0\\). Therefore, \\(E[R_n] / n = E[\\max - \\min + 1] / n \\to 0.6 + 0 + 0 = 0.6\\), corresponding to option I.",
