      "generated_answer": "**A**\n\nThe operator norm of \\(T: L^1[0,2] \\to C[0,2]\\) (with \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty\\)) is 1, achieved by normalized constant functions where \\(\\|Tf\\|_\\infty = 1\\).  \nThe operator norm of \\(T: L^1[0,2] \\to L^1[0,2]\\) is 2, as \\(\\|Tf\\|_1 \\leq \\int_0^2 |f(t)|(2-t)\\,dt \\leq 2\\|f\\|_1\\), with equality approached by functions concentrated near 0.  \nThus, the norms are [1, 2], corresponding to option A.\n\n\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by recalling the operator norm definition: \\(\\|T\\| = \\sup_{\\|f\\|_1 \\leq 1} \\|Tf\\|\\), where norms depend on the codomain.  \n\nFor \\(T: L^1[0,2] \\to C[0,2]\\) with \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty\\), \\(|Tf(x)| = \\left|\\int_0^x f(t)\\,dt\\right| \\leq \\int_0^x |f(t)|\\,dt \\leq \\|f\\|_1 \\leq 1\\), so \\(\\|Tf\\|_\\infty \\leq 1\\). Equality holds for \\(f = 1/2\\) (where \\(\\|f\\|_1 = 1\\)), as \\(Tf(x) = x/2\\) and \\(\\sup |Tf(x)| = 1\\), yielding norm 1.  \n\nFor \\(T: L^1[0,2] \\to L^1[0,2]\\), \\(\\|Tf\\|_1 = \\int_0^2 \\left|\\int_0^x f(t)\\,dt\\right| dx \\leq \\int_0^2 \\int_0^x |f(t)|\\,dt\\, dx = \\int_0^2 |f(t)|(2-t)\\,dt \\leq 2\\|f\\|_1\\), so the norm is at most 2. The bound is achieved in the limit by functions like \\(f_\\epsilon = 1/\\epsilon\\) on \\([0,\\epsilon]\\) (with \\(\\|f_\\epsilon\\|_1 = 1\\)), where \\(\\|Tf_\\epsilon\\|_1 \\to 2\\) as \\(\\epsilon \\to 0\\).  \n\nThus, the norms are [1, 2], matching option A.",
