      "generated_answer": "**I**\n\nA control group should represent the baseline condition without the experimental treatment to isolate its effects. Here, the treatments are specific types of day-long music, so no music at all (option I) best serves as the control by eliminating any music influence. Other options introduce confounding music variables.\n\n\\boxed{I}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by identifying the study's purpose: comparing the effects of day-long classical music versus day-long light rock music on piecework output, requiring a control group that establishes a true baseline. In experimental design, the control must lack the independent variable—here, any music—to measure the treatments' effects accurately. Among the options, only I (no music) achieves this by removing all music exposure, while A through H introduce alternative music types or variations that confound results, and J includes partial music, failing as a pure baseline. Thus, I is selected as the optimal control for isolating the music types' impacts.",
