    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{I}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning evaluates the truth of the two statements using key definitions from abelian group theory.\n\nFirst, Statement 1 is assessed: a free abelian group is a direct sum of copies of ℤ, and ℤ has no non-zero torsion elements, so any element of finite order must be zero, making free abelian groups torsion-free. Thus, Statement 1 is true.\n\nNext, Statement 2 is analyzed: by the fundamental theorem of finitely generated abelian groups, such a group decomposes as ℤ^r ⊕ T, where T is the torsion subgroup; torsion-freeness implies T = 0, so the group is isomorphic to ℤ^r, which is free abelian. Thus, Statement 2 is true.\n\nBoth statements being true corresponds to option I.",
