    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7107",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose the graph of f is both increasing and concave up on a <= x <= b. Then, using the same number of subdivisions, and with L, R, M, and T denoting, respectively, left, right, midpoint, and trapezoid sums, it follows that\n\nOptions: \nA. L <= T <= M <= R\nB. R <= M <= T <= L\nC. M <= L <= T <= R\nD. M <= R <= T <= L\nE. L <= M <= T <= R\nF. T <= L <= R <= M\nG. R <= L <= M <= T\nH. R <= T <= M <= L\nI. T <= R <= M <= L\nJ. L <= R <= T <= M\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**E**\n\nFor an increasing function, left endpoints underestimate midpoint values, which underestimate right endpoints, so \\(L \\leq M \\leq R\\). Concave up implies the midpoint value is at most the trapezoid average by Jensen's inequality, so \\(M \\leq T\\), and \\(T \\leq R\\) since \\(T\\) averages left and right with left smaller. These hold per subinterval and thus for the sums.\n\n\\boxed{E}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by noting that for an increasing function, left endpoints are smaller than midpoint values, which are smaller than right endpoints, yielding L ≤ M ≤ R for the sums. Concave up (convex) implies that the function value at the midpoint is at most the average of the endpoint values by Jensen's inequality, so M ≤ T per subinterval. Additionally, T, as the average of left and right, satisfies T ≤ R because the left value is smaller than the right. These inequalities hold for each subinterval and extend to the total sums, resulting in L ≤ M ≤ T ≤ R, corresponding to option E.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 213,
        "output_tokens": 219,
        "total_tokens": 1875
