    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Statement 1 is false because the kernel of any group homomorphism always contains the identity element and thus cannot be empty.  \nStatement 2 is false because nontrivial homomorphisms exist from finite groups to infinite groups (e.g., $\\mathbb{Z}/2\\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Z}$ via $1 \\mapsto 2$).  \nThus, both are false.\n\n\\boxed{G}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning evaluates each statement against standard group theory definitions.\n\nFirst, Statement 1 claims a homomorphism may have an empty kernel. The kernel is defined as the preimage of the identity in the codomain, which always includes the domain's identity element, making the kernel nonempty. Thus, Statement 1 is false.\n\nNext, Statement 2 claims it is impossible to have a nontrivial homomorphism from a finite group to an infinite group. A counterexample is the injective homomorphism from $\\mathbb{Z}/2\\mathbb{Z}$ (finite) to $\\mathbb{Z}$ (infinite) sending the generator to 2, which is nontrivial as its kernel is trivial. Thus, Statement 2 is false.\n\nBoth statements being false corresponds to option G.",
