      "generated_answer": "Both statements are true. Statement 1 holds because all groups of order \\(p^2\\) (for any prime \\(p\\)) are abelian (either cyclic or elementary abelian). Statement 2 is precisely Sylow's third theorem.\n\n\\boxed{E}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided answer identifies option E (\"True, True\") as correct based on standard results in group theory. First, Statement 1 is true for every prime p, as groups of order p² are classified into two isomorphism classes: the cyclic group ℤ_{p²} and the elementary abelian group ℤ_p × ℤ_p, both of which are abelian. Second, Statement 2 follows directly from Sylow's third theorem, which states that a Sylow p-subgroup of G is normal if and only if it is the unique Sylow p-subgroup, holding for any prime p and group G. Thus, both statements are universally true, matching option E.",
