    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7711",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A kidney dialysis center periodically checks a sample of its equipment and performs a major recalibration if readings are sufficiently off target. Similarly, a fabric factory periodically checks the sizes of towels coming off an assembly line and halts production if measurements are sufficiently off target. In both situations, we have the null hypothesis that the equipment is performing satisfactorily. For each situation, which is the more serious concern, a Type I or Type II error?\n\nOptions: \nA. Dialysis center: Type II error, towel manufacturer: Type II error\nB. Dialysis center: Neither Type I nor Type II error, towel manufacturer: Type II error\nC. Dialysis center: Type I error, towel manufacturer: Type II error\nD. Dialysis center: Type II error, towel manufacturer: Type I error\nE. Dialysis center: Both Type I and Type II errors, towel manufacturer: Type I error\nF. Dialysis center: Type I error, towel manufacturer: Type I error\nG. Dialysis center: Neither Type I nor Type II error, towel manufacturer: Type I error\nH. Dialysis center: Both Type I and Type II errors, towel manufacturer: Type II error\nI. Dialysis center: Type I error, towel manufacturer: Neither Type I nor Type II error\nJ. Dialysis center: Type II error, towel manufacturer: Neither Type I nor Type II error\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Dialysis center:** Type II error is more serious (failing to recalibrate faulty equipment risks patient lives).  \n**Towel manufacturer:** Type I error is more serious (unnecessarily halting production causes costly downtime).  \n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by defining the errors in the context of the null hypothesis (equipment performing satisfactorily): a Type I error occurs when rejecting the null hypothesis incorrectly (declaring equipment faulty when it is not), leading to unnecessary action; a Type II error occurs when failing to reject the null hypothesis incorrectly (declaring equipment satisfactory when it is not), allowing faulty equipment to continue operating.\n\nFor the dialysis center, a Type II error is more serious because it risks patient harm or death from undetected faulty equipment, whereas a Type I error only results in unnecessary recalibration, which is costly but not life-threatening.\n\nFor the towel manufacturer, a Type I error is more serious because it causes production halts and financial losses from downtime, while a Type II error leads to defective products that may be reworked, scrapped, or have less immediate severe economic impact compared to halting the assembly line.",
