    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**F**\n\nThe hologram is recorded with λ_r = 600 nm and reconstructed with λ_c = 500 nm, so the reconstructed image distance scales by the factor λ_c / λ_r = 500/600 = 5/6 relative to the object distance (or original image distance). With the same wavelength (orange), the image distance equals the object distance. Thus, option F is correct.\n\n\\boxed{F}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by identifying the key principle in holography: the reconstructed image distance scales linearly with the ratio of the reconstruction wavelength (λ_c) to the recording wavelength (λ_r). For this case, λ_r = 600 nm (orange) and λ_c = 500 nm (green), yielding the scaling factor λ_c / λ_r = 500/600 = 5/6. When reconstructed with the original orange light, the image distance equals the object distance; therefore, with green light, the reconstructed image distance is (5/6) times the object distance, matching option F. This axial scaling arises because the interference fringe spacing recorded with λ_r diffracts at angles proportional to λ_c during reconstruction, shifting the image position accordingly.",
