      "generated_answer": "**I. Physically unrealizable**\n\nThe speeds are equal when tension \\(T = YA\\), so stress \\(\\sigma = Y\\), corresponding to strain = 1. This exceeds the elastic limit of brass (typically <0.01 strain), making it physically unrealizable. Longitudinal wave speed \\(\\sqrt{Y/\\rho}\\) is independent of frequency, length, temperature, etc.\n\n\\boxed{I}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by recalling the wave speed formulas: transverse wave speed is \\(v_t = \\sqrt{T / \\mu}\\), where \\(\\mu = \\rho A\\), so \\(v_t = \\sqrt{T / (\\rho A)}\\); longitudinal wave speed is \\(v_l = \\sqrt{Y / \\rho}\\). Setting \\(v_t = v_l\\) yields \\(\\sqrt{T / (\\rho A)} = \\sqrt{Y / \\rho}\\), simplifying to \\(T = Y A\\), or stress \\(\\sigma = T / A = Y\\). This stress implies a strain of 1 (100% extension), which exceeds the elastic limit of brass (typically strains below 0.01), rendering the situation physically unrealizable. Among the options, I directly matches this conclusion, as others introduce irrelevant dependencies (e.g., frequency, length) or incorrectly suggest realizability.",
