    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: the calendar of the year 2028 can be used again in the year ?\n\nOptions: \nA. 2041\nB. 1951\nC. 2056\nD. 1971\nE. 1973\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**C**\n\nThe calendars of 2028 and 2056 match because the 28-year interval includes exactly 7 leap days (2028, 2032, 2036, 2040, 2044, 2048, 2052), resulting in 10,227 days, which is divisible by 7. Other options yield remainders when divided by 7 (e.g., 2041 has remainder 3, 1951 has remainder 5). Both years are leap years starting on the same weekday.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by recognizing that calendars repeat when the total number of days between January 1 of two years is a multiple of 7, accounting for both regular days and leap days. For 2028 (a leap year) and 2056, the 28-year difference yields 28 × 365 = 10,220 days, plus 7 leap days (in 2028, 2032, 2036, 2040, 2044, 2048, and 2052), totaling 10,227 days, which divides evenly by 7 (10,227 ÷ 7 = 1,461). Other options fail this test: 2041 (13 years) has 4,749 days (remainder 3); 1951 (77 years back) has 28,124 days (remainder 5); 1971 (57 years back) has 20,819 days (remainder 1); and 1973 (55 years back) has 20,088 days (remainder 5). Thus, only 2056 matches the calendar of 2028 exactly.",
