    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The real line under the K-topology is Hausdorff and first countable, but is neither regular nor normal.\n\n\\boxed{first countable}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning identifies the K-topology on the real line, as defined in standard topology texts like Munkres, where the basis consists of usual open intervals together with sets of the form (a, b) \\ K, and K = {1/n : n = 1, 2, ...}. This topology is Hausdorff because it refines the standard topology on ℝ, which is Hausdorff. It is first countable, as each point has a countable local basis derived from rational-endpoint intervals and punctured versions avoiding K. However, it fails regularity because the closed set K cannot be separated from the point 0 by disjoint open sets: any open neighborhood of 0, such as (-1, 1) \\ K, intersects every open neighborhood of points in K near 0. Consequently, the space is neither regular nor normal, aligning with the statement and confirming \"first countable\" as the specified property.",
      "success": true,
